START TRANSACTION;
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='tips-before-cesarean-delivery', `title_en`='Tips Before a Cesarean Delivery', `meta_title_en`='Tips Before Cesarean Delivery | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='Helpful tips before a cesarean delivery to ease your mind: how many C-sections are allowed, the steps, benefits, risks, and when a C-section is necessary.', `excerpt_en`='You may need a few reassuring tips before your cesarean delivery. In this article you will find everything you need to know and more, from the steps of the procedure to its benefits and risks.', `tag_en`='Cesarean', `image_alt_en`='Pregnant woman preparing for a cesarean delivery procedure — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>You may need a few tips before your cesarean delivery that will, without a doubt, put your mind at ease. In this article you will find everything you need and more.</p><h2>How many cesarean deliveries are allowed?</h2><p>The number of cesarean deliveries allowed differs for each mother according to the condition of the mother and the fetus and the surrounding health circumstances. In many cases a cesarean is a medically necessary option to preserve the safety of the mother and the fetus. There are also recommendations in some countries and health organizations that set a limit on the number of cesarean deliveries allowed for mothers. For example, the World Health Organization recommends that the cesarean rate in any country should not exceed 10-15% of total births. Nevertheless, it is essential to consult the treating medical team to assess the health condition and make the appropriate decision about the mode of delivery, so as to reach the best decision for her and for the fetus.</p><h2>Steps of a cesarean delivery</h2><p>Here are the steps of a cesarean delivery with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk: 1. Preparation: the mother is prepared for the operation through specific recommendations, including a fasting period before the operation and fluid intake beforehand. 2. Anesthesia: the mother is given regional anesthesia, general anesthesia, or another type depending on the recommendation of the medical team and the mother''s health condition. 3. An incision is made in the upper abdominal wall, then the uterus is reached after opening the abdominal wall and surrounding tissues to access the uterus. 4. The uterus is opened to reach the fetus, and then the baby is delivered from the uterus. 5. After the baby is delivered, the wound and surrounding tissues are closed with medical sutures that leave no mark after delivery. 6. After the operation the mother is transferred to a dedicated recovery room under observation.</p><h2>Benefits of a cesarean delivery for the vagina</h2><p>Some of the potential benefits of a cesarean delivery for the vagina include: 1. Protecting the vagina and genital area: a cesarean delivery may protect the vagina and genital tissues from the tearing or injury that can occur during a natural birth. 2. A cesarean delivery may play a role in reducing the risk of anal tearing that may occur in some cases during a natural birth. 3. A cesarean delivery can be scheduled for a specific date in consultation with the medical team, compared with a natural birth that may start naturally at any time. 4. In some cases a cesarean delivery may have a positive effect on the safety and health of the baby, especially when there are health problems or special circumstances that require a cesarean delivery.</p><p>Nevertheless, it should be noted that a cesarean delivery is not the healthiest option for all women; rather, the decision to have a cesarean delivery should be made based on the recommendation of Dr. Ahmed Marzouk and an assessment of the health condition of the mother and fetus.</p><h2>When is a cesarean delivery necessary?</h2><p>A cesarean delivery is necessary in several medical situations, such as the following: 1. Difficulties with natural birth: there may be difficulties in the natural birth process that make a cesarean delivery the better option, such as a large fetal size or an incorrect fetal position. 2. In cases where there are health problems that increase the risks of natural birth for the mother, a cesarean delivery may be performed to protect the mother''s health and safety, such as high blood pressure during pregnancy or heart or lung problems. 3. When there are problems in the uterus that make natural birth impossible or dangerous, the decision is made to perform a cesarean delivery, such as the presence of scars on the uterus (from previous cesarean operations) or the presence of tumors in the uterus. 4. In some cases, certain emergency circumstances may require a cesarean delivery to preserve the safety of the mother and fetus, such as severe bleeding or a rapid deterioration in the condition of the fetus.</p><h2>What are the risks of a cesarean delivery?</h2><p>Although a cesarean delivery is generally considered a safe surgical procedure, it does carry some risks and challenges. The risks of a cesarean delivery may include the following: 1. When general anesthesia is used for the cesarean delivery, complications from the anesthesia can occur, such as allergic reactions, difficulty breathing, low blood pressure, and heart problems. 2. Some general surgical risks such as infection, bleeding, collection of blood in the wound, formation of blood clots, and injury to adjacent organs such as the bladder or intestines. 3. The wound resulting from the cesarean delivery may experience problems with healing, which increases the risk of infection or the formation of a large tissue scar. 4. There is a higher risk of blood clots after a cesarean delivery compared with a natural birth, and the mother may also face psychological effects after a cesarean delivery, such as depression, anxiety, or difficulty coping with the surgical birth experience.</p><h2>Which is more dangerous, natural birth or cesarean?</h2><p>There is no clear answer as to which is more dangerous between natural birth and cesarean delivery, as each has its own disadvantages and advantages, as follows:</p><p>Natural birth:</p><ul><li>The vagina and genital tissues may be exposed to tears or injuries during natural birth.</li><li>Greater bleeding can occur after natural birth.</li><li>The recovery period after natural birth may take less time.</li></ul><p>Cesarean delivery:</p><ul><li>It involves a surgical procedure and the use of general anesthesia.</li><li>It carries surgical risks such as infection, bleeding, and blood collection.</li><li>It can increase the risk of blood clotting.</li><li>It requires a longer recovery period and follow-up care after delivery.</li></ul><p>It is important to emphasize that these risks differ from one case to another, and each woman''s condition must be assessed by Dr. Ahmed Marzouk to make the appropriate decision about the mode of delivery. The mother should also talk with him and discuss the risks and benefits of each available option, as well as when a cesarean delivery becomes dangerous, whether the vagina widens after a cesarean delivery, how it is determined whether induced labor or a cesarean operation is better for her, and what a pain-free cesarean delivery is and whether we can resort to it or not.</p>' WHERE `id`='d939db12-25b8-5e79-92f3-f5b6ddfef6b8';
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='uterine-fibroid-treatment', `title_en`='Uterine Fibroid Treatment', `meta_title_en`='Uterine Fibroid Treatment | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='Learn about uterine fibroid treatment, pregnancy rates after fibroid removal, how long the surgery takes, its risks, and bleeding after the operation.', `excerpt_en`='Is treating a uterine fibroid easy, and how is it done? This article covers uterine fibroid treatment, the chances of pregnancy after removal, and the other questions women with fibroids often ask.', `tag_en`='Fibroids', `image_alt_en`='Illustration of a uterine fibroid inside the uterus — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>Is treating a uterine fibroid easy, and how is it done? These are the foremost questions on the mind of anyone diagnosed with uterine fibroids, along with the risks of removing them. In this article we will learn together about uterine fibroid treatment and the chances of pregnancy after removal, alongside some of the other matters that may cross your mind about uterine fibroids.</p><h2>Pregnancy rate after fibroid removal</h2><p>The pregnancy rate after fibroid removal depends on several factors, including the size of the tumor, the shape and location of the uterine fibroid, the surgical method used, and the woman''s general health. In many cases a woman can conceive naturally after fibroid removal. However, the following factors, which directly influence pregnancy, must be taken into account: 1. The location and size of the fibroid: if the fibroid is large or close to the uterine lining or the placenta, this may affect the ability to conceive and to maintain the pregnancy if it occurs. 2. The condition of the uterus: sometimes removing the fibroid may require surgical procedures that affect the structure of the uterus itself. This may affect the uterus''s ability to maintain a pregnancy. 3. A previous pregnancy: if you have a history of a failed pregnancy, this will affect the ability to conceive after the operation. 4. Your general health: a woman''s overall health plays a role in her ability to conceive and to carry a pregnancy to term successfully.</p><h2>Fibroid treatment</h2><p>Fibroid treatment depends on several factors, including the size of the tumor, its location, the symptoms associated with it, and the desire for a future pregnancy. In general, fibroid treatment may include the following: 1. Regular monitoring: in the case of small fibroids that do not cause significant symptoms, the most appropriate option may be to examine the fibroid regularly to monitor its progress. This can be suitable if there are no serious health problems. 2. Medication: in some cases doctors may use medication to control the symptoms of the fibroid, such as heavy bleeding, which can help reduce the size of the tumor as well as reduce the bleeding. 3. Surgery: in the case of large fibroids or those causing severe symptoms, surgery may be required to remove the tumor, and in some serious cases doctors may resort to surgery to remove the uterus itself.</p><h2>How long does fibroid removal surgery take?</h2><p>The duration of fibroid removal surgery varies based on several factors, including the size of the tumor, its location, the surgical method used, and the complexity of the condition itself. In general, this surgery may take from about 30 minutes to approximately two hours.</p><h2>Risks of fibroid removal</h2><p>Fibroid removal surgery may be safe and effective in most cases, but like any surgical operation it can result in risks and complications. Here are some of the potential risks of fibroid removal surgery: 1. Bleeding: like any surgery, bleeding may occur during or after the fibroid removal operation. The bleeding may be mild or severe depending on the case itself. 2. Infection: an infection may occur at the surgical site, so it is important to take infection-prevention measures and follow the instructions of Dr. Ahmed Marzouk after the operation. 3. Damage to organs adjacent to the tumor site: there may be risks of harming organs adjacent to the tumor site, such as the intestines or the bladder. 4. Uterine deformity: in some cases fibroid removal may lead to a change in the shape or structure of the uterus, and this may affect the ability to conceive afterward. 5. Anesthesia complications: the anesthesia process may cause rare complications such as allergic reactions to the medications used or breathing problems. 6. Scar formation: scars may form in the surgical area. These scars may be temporary or permanent and may affect the appearance of the abdomen. 7. Fluid collection: fluid may collect in the surgical area, causing abdominal swelling after the fibroid removal operation, and this may require additional medical intervention.</p><h2>Bleeding after a fibroid operation</h2><p>Bleeding may occur after a fibroid removal operation, and bleeding after surgery is normal and not a cause for concern. However, if the bleeding is severe or continues for a long time, or if you feel any abnormal symptoms such as increased pain, redness, or swelling at the wound site, you must contact Dr. Ahmed Marzouk immediately. Here are some of the possible causes of bleeding after surgery: 1. Slight bleeding may occur at the wound site where the fibroid was removed. This bleeding can cause noticeable leakage of blood. 2. Some people may experience slight blood clotting after surgery as a result of certain medical measures taken to reduce bleeding during the operation. 3. Swelling in the wound area may cause a collection of fluid and blood, which can cause slight blood leakage. 4. Some women may experience slight bleeding after surgery due to the effect of hormones or the menstrual cycle after fibroid removal.</p><p>Dr. Ahmed Marzouk has witnessed the best experiences for women with fibroids, and he has treated many, many cases of fibroids at the best cost for a fibroid removal operation in Egypt, with which you can treat a uterine fibroid easily and with the highest possible efficiency.</p>' WHERE `id`='b828ea52-c4aa-5277-be64-3c8f85e03054';
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='egg-freezing', `title_en`='Egg Freezing: A Complete Guide', `meta_title_en`='Egg Freezing | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='Everything about egg freezing: the best age, how the procedure works, when to consider it, risks, success rates, safety, and the cost of freezing your eggs.', `excerpt_en`='As marriage is delayed for many women, worries about fertility grow. Egg freezing has emerged as a safe solution: eggs are retrieved and frozen until the right time to conceive arrives.', `tag_en`='Fertility', `image_alt_en`='Egg freezing procedure preserving eggs in a fertility laboratory — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>With marriage being delayed for many young women in recent times, many of them have developed fears of not being able to conceive. From here the technique of egg freezing emerged as a safe solution to this problem, in which the eggs are preserved by retrieving them from the woman''s body and freezing them until the appropriate time to conceive is available.</p><h2>What is the most suitable age for egg freezing, and how long can a frozen egg be kept?</h2><p>The ovarian reserve declines after a woman reaches the age of thirty-five, and the quality of the eggs gradually decreases until reaching menopause, while the chance of a successful pregnancy declines and the odds of having a baby with birth defects increase. For this reason, many women resort to freezing 10 eggs or more before reaching this age. Some women resort to egg freezing at the age of forty, but the most suitable age remains from the late twenties up to the age of thirty-five, because the eggs retain their quality then. The quality of the eggs can be preserved for up to 10 years after the freezing procedure.</p><h2>When should you resort to egg freezing?</h2><ul><li>If you suffer from genetic diseases that affect the quality of the eggs or cause infertility.</li><li>If you suffer from autoimmune diseases such as lupus, or blood diseases such as sickle cell anemia.</li><li>If you are a cancer patient undergoing chemotherapy or radiotherapy, which may affect your ability to conceive in the future.</li><li>If you are thinking of postponing marriage and pregnancy until after forty for educational or social reasons.</li><li>If you wish to preserve the extra eggs after an in vitro fertilization (IVF) procedure.</li></ul><h2>How is egg freezing done?</h2><p>The egg freezing process is divided into several stages, starting with the preparatory stage, then the ovulation-stimulation stage, then egg retrieval, and then freezing.</p><p>1. The preparation stage for egg freezing: the doctor performs tests to check for any infectious diseases such as hepatitis and AIDS, tests to detect hormone levels such as ovarian reserve (AMH), follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), and estradiol, and an ultrasound examination to assess the condition of the ovaries and determine the number of mature eggs. He may also request an egg-reserve test or an ovarian-reserve test.</p><p>2. The ovulation-stimulation stage: after reading the ovarian-reserve test, the doctor gives a set of medications to stop the menstrual cycle, then hormonal treatments to stimulate the ovaries to produce more eggs. He then carries out blood tests and an ultrasound examination to monitor the egg-production process.</p><p>3. The egg-retrieval stage: the doctor retrieves the eggs, which number between 5 and 25, using a small needle connected to an ultrasound probe through the vagina. This procedure is done under the effect of anesthesia using pain-relieving injections. The procedure lasts about 20 minutes, after which the woman can leave the hospital.</p><p>4. The egg-freezing stage: after the eggs are retrieved, they are frozen in the embryology laboratory at below-zero temperatures until the woman wishes to conceive.</p><h2>How are frozen eggs used to achieve pregnancy?</h2><p>The frozen eggs are thawed and then fertilized with sperm using in vitro fertilization, and then implanted in the mother''s uterus. After the success of this process, pregnancy symptoms appear on the woman, and she needs complete rest.</p><h2>What are the possible risks of egg freezing?</h2><p>The egg freezing process may cause some rare harms, including the following:</p><ul><li>The puncture needle used to extract the eggs may cause bleeding or infection in the woman.</li><li>Pain in the ovary as a result of the hormonal treatment injections.</li><li>Some minor complications from the anesthesia used during the egg-retrieval procedure.</li><li>The possibility of some side effects resulting from ovarian stimulation, such as vomiting, nausea, diarrhea, bloating, and abdominal pain.</li><li>If the frozen eggs are used for pregnancy at a late age, the chance of pregnancy failure and miscarriage increases.</li></ul><h2>What is the ruling on egg freezing, and is it permissible (halal)?</h2><p>The Egyptian Dar al-Ifta has permitted egg freezing if its purpose is to preserve offspring, as it is considered a complement to the IVF process, and scholars have permitted it under the following conditions:</p><ul><li>The frozen fertilized egg must be placed only in the uterus of the egg''s owner, and it is not permitted to place it in the uterus of another woman.</li><li>The frozen egg must be kept in a safe place and must not be mixed with other eggs, to prevent the mixing of lineages.</li><li>The motive for performing the egg freezing procedure must be legitimate.</li><li>The fertilization of the frozen egg must be done with the sperm of the husband only, and the marriage must be in place at the time of fertilization.</li></ul><h2>How much does egg freezing cost?</h2><p>The required cost varies according to:</p><ul><li>The tests needed to monitor the ovulation process and to check for infectious diseases.</li><li>The medications used to stimulate the ovaries, as well as the price of the ovulation-related tests such as the price of the ovarian-reserve test or the egg-reserve test.</li><li>The number of ultrasound sessions.</li><li>The cost of storing the eggs in the egg bank and the freezing technique used.</li></ul><h2>Is egg freezing a safe procedure?</h2><p>Egg freezing is a safe procedure and has no connection to an increased risk of the fetus having birth defects or malformations, and it does not increase pregnancy complications compared with a natural pregnancy.</p><h2>The success rate of egg freezing, and does pregnancy occur with a low ovarian reserve?</h2><p>The success rate of egg freezing is high, but the success rate of the in vitro fertilization that uses the frozen eggs varies according to several factors, including the woman''s age, the condition of the egg at retrieval and whether it was frozen after or before fertilization, as well as the variation in the normal level of the egg-reserve hormone. The egg reserve at the age of forty differs from the reserve at thirty, and likewise at twenty years of age, and whether stimulation affects the egg reserve or not, and so on.</p><p>In the end, if you are afraid of getting older and you want to preserve your chance of conceiving through egg freezing, you can turn to the egg freezing technique without any fear of losing your chance to conceive at all.</p>' WHERE `id`='0726bf7a-c819-5e37-bf9c-448af6e5238c';
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='uterine-septum', `title_en`='Uterine Septum: Symptoms and Treatment', `meta_title_en`='Uterine Septum | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='Learn about the uterine septum: its symptoms, causes, treatment options, the surgical procedure, and when pregnancy can occur after septum removal.', `excerpt_en`='A uterine septum is a thin layer of tissue that divides the uterus into two halves due to a congenital defect. Learn its symptoms, causes, treatment, and when pregnancy is possible after removal.', `tag_en`='Uterine Septum', `image_alt_en`='Diagram of a uterine septum dividing the uterus — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>The uterine septum is a thin layer of tissue located between the vagina and the uterus. It extends along the length of the uterus and divides it into two halves as a result of a congenital defect during formation. The uterine septum is among the topics that interest many people, so what problems can affect it? And when does pregnancy occur after removal of the uterine septum?</p><h2>Symptoms that, when they appear, indicate you have a uterine septum</h2><p>There are some symptoms that may indicate the presence of a uterine septum dividing the uterus into two halves, among these symptoms: 1. Abnormal bleeding: some people may experience abnormal bleeding after intercourse or after a medical cervical examination, so if you suffer from abnormal or persistent bleeding, there may be a tear or a problem in your uterine septum. 2. Pain during intercourse: you may feel pain or tension in the pelvic area during intercourse, and this may be a sign of problems with the uterine septum. 3. Recurrent infections in the vagina or uterus: if you suffer from recurrent infections in the vagina or uterus, the uterine septum may be the cause. 4. Recurrent miscarriages: the uterine septum may play a role in cases of recurrent miscarriages, if there is a weakness in the uterine septum or in the case of malformations in it.</p><h2>Are there causes for the uterine septum problem?</h2><p>There can be several causes for the appearance of a uterine septum, including: 1. Congenital malformations: the uterine septum may be underdeveloped or malformed from birth, and these congenital malformations may be the result of a defect in embryonic development. 2. Pelvic infections: some chronic pelvic inflammations or bacterial infections may lead to the formation of scars or fibrosis in the uterine area, causing a uterine septum.</p><p>Therefore, the specialist doctor must assess each patient''s condition individually to determine the possible causes of the appearance of the uterine septum and provide the appropriate treatment for her.</p><h2>Is there a treatment for the uterine septum problem?</h2><p>Yes, there is a treatment for the uterine septum problem depending on the cause and severity of the problem, including the following: 1. Surgical operations: in some cases, treatment of the uterine septum may require a surgical operation through which the deformed parts of the septum that prevent pregnancy are removed. 2. Hormonal therapy: hormonal therapy can be used in some cases to treat the uterine septum problem. 3. Vaginal dilation techniques: vaginal dilation techniques can be used to help resolve the uterine septum problem.</p><h2>The uterine septum operation</h2><p>It is a surgical procedure in which the uterine septum is removed or corrected when it is causing a health problem or hindering pregnancy. The uterine septum operation may include the following steps: 1. The doctor conducts a comprehensive assessment of your health condition, reviewing the complete medical history and performing some specialized medical examinations, such as a pelvic exam, ultrasound imaging, and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), to determine the size and shape of the uterine septum and to find out whether there are any accompanying problems. 2. An appointment for the operation is arranged after explaining the necessary instructions for preparing for it, such as fasting before surgery and instructions regarding medications and other required preparations. 3. The operation is performed under general anesthesia and usually takes from one to two hours, during which the uterine septum is reached through the vaginal route (hysteroscopy) or through the abdomen, then the deformed or damaged parts of the uterine septum are removed and the uterus is reconstructed again. 4. After the operation the patient is transferred to the recovery room where she is monitored for a short period, and she will also be informed of some rest and care instructions needed after the operation so that she passes through a recovery period in complete safety.</p><h2>When does pregnancy occur after removal of the uterine septum?</h2><p>Pregnancy is possible after removal of the uterine septum and can occur at any time after the operation. However, the time the body takes to regain its ability to heal differs from one woman to another, and many couples may require some time for pregnancy to occur after removal of the uterine septum, while others may conceive in the first months right after the operation.</p><p>In general, when the uterine septum removal operation is resorted to, couples must follow the medical directions provided by Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, and it is usually advised to wait for some time before trying to conceive, in order to allow the body to heal completely after the operation.</p><p>Every woman is different from another, and the exact timing of pregnancy after removal of the uterine septum depends on many factors, such as the health of the couple and their fertility rate, among other factors. Therefore, if you wish to become pregnant after undergoing a uterine septum removal operation, it is important to talk to your treating doctor to obtain specific information and guidance for your personal condition and to determine whether the presence of the uterine septum will affect your chances of pregnancy or not.</p>' WHERE `id`='36707b39-19ae-5848-b868-b1873ad7e79b';
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='tips-after-cesarean-delivery', `title_en`='Tips After a Cesarean Delivery', `meta_title_en`='Tips After Cesarean Delivery | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='Essential tips after a cesarean delivery: the right sleeping position, warning signs, foods to avoid, pain relief, bathing, and breastfeeding after a C-section.', `excerpt_en`='Many women look for tips after a cesarean delivery. Learn the most important ones in this article, covering sleeping positions, warning signs, foods to avoid, pain relief, and bathing.', `tag_en`='Cesarean', `image_alt_en`='New mother resting and recovering after a cesarean delivery — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>Many women look for some tips after a cesarean delivery. Learn about the most important ones in the following article.</p><h2>The correct sleeping position after a cesarean delivery</h2><p>After a cesarean delivery, it is advised to follow these guidelines for proper sleep: 1. It is preferable to sleep on your side rather than on your back, as this will be more comfortable for you and will help reduce pressure on the wound, especially with pain in the right side of the abdomen after a cesarean delivery during the postpartum (nifas) period. 2. You can use extra pillows to support your body and relieve pressure on the wound; a small pillow can be placed under the abdomen and another pillow between the knees to improve the sleeping position. 3. The head of the bed can be raised slightly using pillows to raise the upper part of the body, as this will help reduce swelling and contribute to a comfortable sleep. 4. It is advised to avoid standing or sitting for long periods in the early period after the cesarean operation, but you should rest and lie down regularly to shorten the recovery period after delivery. 5. Make sure to follow the instructions of Dr. Ahmed Marzouk for the wound after delivery, as the doctor may recommend placing a clean dressing on the wound and changing it regularly. 6. If you feel difficulty moving or need extra help, ask your husband to help you change your sleeping position and carry out daily activities on your behalf.</p><h2>Warning signs after a cesarean delivery</h2><p>If you notice any of the following signs after a cesarean delivery, you must go to the emergency department or call the doctor immediately: 1. Heavy bleeding: if you experience severe and abnormal bleeding after the cesarean operation, this may indicate an emergency problem requiring immediate intervention, such as internal bleeding. 2. Severe pain or swelling in the legs: if you have sharp pain in the legs or abnormal swelling, this may be a sign of blood clotting in the blood vessels. 3. A rise in temperature: if you have a fever after a cesarean delivery, this fever may indicate an infection in the surgical wound or another infection. 4. Difficulty breathing or chest pain: if you experience difficulty breathing, sharp chest pain, or shortness of breath, these may be signs of serious respiratory problems such as a pulmonary embolism. 5. An increase in bleeding or a change in yellowing: if the bleeding increases or you notice a change in the yellow color of the skin or eyes, this may be a sign of liver problems or internal bleeding. Contact us immediately if you feel any of the previous symptoms.</p><p>Rest completely assured that such complications happen with an inexperienced doctor. With Dr. Ahmed Marzouk we have many years of experience and a medical team to provide the necessary follow-up at the highest level.</p><h2>Foods forbidden after a cesarean delivery</h2><p>You should avoid exercising after a cesarean delivery for a while, and there are also some foods that may be avoided after a cesarean delivery to avoid some potential problems such as gas, bloating, and irritation of the digestive system. Here are some examples of foods that are forbidden or preferably avoided for a period after a cesarean delivery: 1. Heavy and fatty foods: avoid heavy and fatty foods that are difficult to digest and that may cause bloating and a feeling of heaviness, such as fast food and fried foods. 2. Spicy foods and strong seasonings: spicy foods and strong seasonings can increase the likelihood of heartburn or digestive irritation, so it may be best to avoid them or reduce their amount in meals. 3. Gas-producing foods: some foods and drinks such as beans, lentils, and carbonated drinks can cause an increase in gas and bloating, so it may be best to avoid them in the early period after the cesarean operation. 4. Caffeine and alcohol: it is preferable to reduce the consumption of drinks containing caffeine or alcohol, as they can affect your sleep and affect the fluid balance in the body; instead, keep drinking beverages that cleanse the uterus after a cesarean delivery, such as anise, turmeric with milk, and others. 5. Preserved and processed foods: preserved and processed foods contain preservatives and chemicals that may sometimes be harmful, so it is preferable to eat fresh foods prepared at home rather than canned or processed foods, as they are a better laxative for constipation after a cesarean delivery, especially vegetables.</p><h2>PCA injection after a cesarean delivery</h2><p>After a cesarean delivery, a PCA (Patient-Controlled Analgesia) device is used to reduce pain effectively, as the PCA device allows the patient to control the doses of painkillers according to her need. This means the patient can press the button to deliver a dose of painkiller through the catheter or the tube connected to the patient''s vein, to reduce her feeling of pain as much as possible after delivery, making it the best painkiller after a cesarean delivery.</p><h2>Causes of the absence of postpartum bleeding after a cesarean delivery</h2><p>After a cesarean delivery, some women may experience a delay in postpartum (nifas) bleeding, or they may experience a complete stop in the bleeding. Among the possible causes of this situation are the following: 1. Weak uterine contractions: weak uterine contractions may occur after a cesarean delivery, leading to reduced bleeding or a complete stop. 2. A decrease in hormone levels: performing the cesarean may lead to a decrease in the secretion of the hormones that play a role in postpartum bleeding, such as estrogen, and therefore the onset of bleeding may be delayed due to this decrease. 3. Blood collection inside the uterus: blood may collect inside the uterus due to blood pooling at the site of the cesarean surgery, and this collection may lead to the absence of normal bleeding. 4. Uterine inflammation: inflammation of the uterus may occur after a cesarean delivery, affecting postpartum bleeding, and this inflammation may be the result of an infection or a collection of fluids inside the uterus.</p><h2>When does the newborn breastfeed after a cesarean delivery?</h2><p>Usually the newborn is able to breastfeed immediately after a cesarean delivery in the delivery room or in the operating room when the mother is in good and stable condition. In general, however, the first breastfeeding after a cesarean delivery differs slightly from a natural birth and may take a longer time. The timing of the first breastfeeding can be affected by some factors such as the mother''s health condition and the anesthesia used. Nevertheless, it is preferable to place the newborn beside the mother and have her attempt to breastfeed him to encourage natural breastfeeding, as the newborn may need some help with the correct breastfeeding position at first, and our nurses can provide you with support and advice wholeheartedly.</p><h2>Is bathing allowed after a cesarean delivery?</h2><p>Yes, after a cesarean delivery the mother is usually allowed to bathe within a period ranging from 24 to 48 hours, but this can differ according to the recommendation of Dr. Ahmed Marzouk and the mother''s health condition. It is also advised to observe these tips after a cesarean delivery for bathing: 1. Follow the medical team''s instructions: follow the guidance provided by our nurses about when to bathe after a cesarean delivery. 2. Use warm water: it is preferable to use warm, not hot, water for bathing, as warm water may help you relax and relieve feelings of tension. 3. Avoid massaging the surgical area forcefully during bathing, as you should avoid using a loofah or vigorous scrubbing on the surgical area until it heals completely. 4. It is advised to gently wash the area adjacent to the wound using water and a suitable soap and to dry it well after bathing. 5. Monitor the wound and the surrounding area after bathing, and if you notice any unusual changes such as severe redness, swelling, or fluid leakage, you must contact us immediately.</p>' WHERE `id`='7002bc53-f260-5fe4-8bf8-35a974682816';
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='uterine-fibroids', `title_en`='Uterine Fibroids: Everything You Need to Know', `meta_title_en`='Uterine Fibroids Guide | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='Learn the symptoms of uterine fibroids, how they differ from uterine cancer, and when their size is dangerous, with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, OB-GYN specialist.', `excerpt_en`='Being diagnosed with fibroids can be frightening, especially a breast or uterine fibroid. Discover the symptoms of uterine fibroids, how they differ from cancer, and when a fibroid becomes dangerous, with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, OB-GYN specialist.', `tag_en`='Fibroids', `image_alt_en`='Illustration of uterine fibroids in the wall of the uterus — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>Being diagnosed with fibroids is one of the most difficult things a person can face, especially in the case of a breast fibroid or a uterine fibroid, which have become widespread among many women recently. In this article, therefore, we will cover the symptoms of uterine fibroids and everything you want to know about fibroids with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, obstetrics and gynecology specialist.</p><h2>Symptoms of uterine fibroids</h2><p>A uterine fibroid is a type of fibroid that develops in the wall of the uterus. There may be a single fibroid or several fibroids at the same time, and the causes, types, and symptoms of fibroids differ from one person to another and depend on the size and location of the fibroid. Here are some symptoms that may be associated with a uterine fibroid: 1. Abnormal bleeding: there is a strong relationship between fibroids and the menstrual cycle, as a fibroid may increase menstrual bleeding. The blood may be heavier, and the period longer or more frequent than usual. 2. Pelvic pain: some women may feel pain in the pelvic area or lower back. This pain may be constant or worsen during the menstrual period. 3. Abdominal enlargement: if there is a large fibroid in the uterus, it may lead to abdominal enlargement or a feeling of fullness. 4. A mass in the abdomen: you may feel a lump or swelling in the abdominal area, which may be due to the size of the fibroid. 5. Pressure on neighboring organs: a large uterine fibroid may press on nearby organs such as the bladder, which may cause frequent urination or difficulty urinating. 6. Constipation or diarrhea: in some cases, a fibroid may press on the intestines, which can affect bowel movements. 7. Pain during intercourse: some women may feel pain during intercourse due to the fibroid and its effect on the pelvic area.</p><h2>The difference between uterine fibroids and uterine cancer</h2><p>Here is the difference between uterine fibroids and uterine cancer. Uterine fibroids: 1. A fibroid is a benign (non-cancerous) tumor that grows in the muscle of the uterine wall. 2. A fibroid is made up of muscle cells, which make up the normal size of the fibroid. 3. Symptoms may include heavy bleeding during the menstrual period, pelvic pain, an enlarged uterus, and other symptoms related to the location and size of the fibroid. 4. Small fibroids are often left alone if they do not cause significant symptoms. In some cases, a fibroid can be surgically removed if it causes health problems or serious symptoms.</p><p>Uterine cancer (cancer of the uterus): 1. Uterine cancer is a cancerous condition in which the cells in the lining of the uterus turn into dangerous cancerous cells. 2. Uterine cancer can grow and spread to other areas of the uterus and beyond, and it is classified into different types depending on its cell forms. 3. Symptoms may include abnormal bleeding between periods or after menopause, pelvic pain, unexplained weight loss, and other symptoms associated with uterine cancer. 4. Treatment depends on the stage and type of uterine cancer, and may include surgery to remove the tumor, radiation therapy, or chemotherapy.</p><h2>Do fibroids go away?</h2><p>Yes, in some cases a fibroid can disappear naturally without the need for special treatment. However, this depends on several factors, including the size of the fibroid, its location, the symptoms associated with it, and its effect on the person''s quality of life. Small fibroids that do not cause strong symptoms may disappear on their own or may persist without causing significant problems. In addition, the size of fibroids can change over time and with hormonal changes, such as during the premenstrual phase or during pregnancy.</p><p>Nevertheless, a fibroid should be evaluated by Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, obstetrics and gynecology physician, to confirm its nature and determine whether it requires medical intervention or not.</p><h2>When is a fibroid''s size dangerous?</h2><p>The size of a fibroid is not the only factor that determines how dangerous it is. A fibroid may be small yet cause severe symptoms, while it may be large and have no effect on health at all. Rather, the seriousness of a fibroid depends on the following: 1. If the fibroid causes severe symptoms such as heavy bleeding, persistent pelvic pain, or a negative effect on quality of life in general, it may be considered dangerous regardless of its size. 2. The location of the fibroid can play a role in determining its seriousness. If the fibroid is close to vital organs such as the bladder or intestines, it may have a greater impact. 3. If the fibroid is growing rapidly or changing noticeably, this may be an indicator of seriousness, as such rapid changes may point to abnormal development. 4. In the case of women who wish to become pregnant, the size and location of the fibroid may affect the ability to conceive and maintain a pregnancy. 5. If there are other chronic diseases that make dealing with the fibroid more complicated, this may be an important indicator of the seriousness of the fibroid.</p><p>Therefore, it is important to have the fibroid checked regularly by Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, obstetrics and gynecology physician, to assess the condition of the fibroid and determine the appropriate steps to deal with it, especially if you have any doubts about the fibroid or suffer from abnormal symptoms associated with it, which may require you to undergo surgery for removal. Ask him all the questions on your mind, such as: how long does fibroid removal surgery take? Do fibroids grow? Do fibroids come out with the menstrual period? Do fibroids disappear after menopause? And do fibroids cause abdominal bloating or not?</p>' WHERE `id`='a8966dbd-d32a-51d7-bb8d-84e667a53752';
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='epidural-for-cesarean-delivery', `title_en`='Epidural Injection for a Cesarean Delivery', `meta_title_en`='Epidural for Cesarean | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='Discover how the epidural enables a painless cesarean delivery, how it is given, its benefits, harms, and contraindications, with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, OB-GYN.', `excerpt_en`='Fear of pain and general anesthesia worries many women before a cesarean. Learn how the epidural back injection enables a painless cesarean delivery, how it is given, and its benefits and harms, with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk.', `tag_en`='Cesarean', `image_alt_en`='Anesthesiologist giving an epidural back injection before a cesarean delivery — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>Pain remains one of the main fears women experience with a cesarean delivery, alongside their fear of general anesthesia and its complications. This is why painless cesarean delivery using a back injection (epidural) appeared. It is a type of regional anesthesia in which the mother stays awake throughout the stages of the cesarean delivery but does not feel pain, and it also helps reduce the amount of anesthetic drugs that reach the fetus. It is injected into the lower back, causing loss of sensation in the area around the abdomen and the entire pelvic region.</p><p>There is also more than one type of back injection, including the epidural injection (epidural anesthesia) and the spinal injection (spinal cord fluid anesthesia).</p><p>Many women ask: what is the back injection for natural birth? And about the benefits and harms of the epidural for natural birth. The epidural is used in natural birth to relieve labor pain, and epidural experiences have achieved great success in relieving natural birth pain when a woman fears the pain, as it relieves pain in the lower half of the body while keeping the mother able to push to help with delivery. In cesarean deliveries, either a spinal or an epidural is used.</p><h2>Is the back injection the same as spinal anesthesia?</h2><ul><li>Spinal anesthesia works faster than the epidural because the anesthetic enters the spinal cord directly and starts taking effect within 5 minutes, and it does not require the injection to remain in the body like the epidural.</li><li>The epidural numbs the lower half of the woman''s body while keeping her able to push in the case of natural birth, whereas spinal anesthesia numbs the muscles and the woman cannot push to help with natural birth, which is why spinal anesthesia is not used in natural birth.</li><li>Spinal anesthesia is not used in cases of heart disease and respiratory system disease.</li></ul><h2>How is the back injection given for delivery?</h2><ul><li>The anesthesiologist asks you to sit or lie on your side.</li><li>He sterilizes the back area and injects a local anesthetic to reduce the pain of inserting the back needle.</li><li>The doctor inserts a hollow needle between the vertebrae, then passes the catheter tube through the needle.</li><li>The needle is removed and the catheter tube is secured to the back.</li><li>The catheter is used to deliver medications to relieve pain.</li><li>The doctor monitors blood pressure and oxygen, and the heartbeats of the mother and fetus.</li><li>The effect of the back injection wears off several hours after the catheter is removed.</li><li>The doctor prefers that the patient walk inside the room to relieve numbness in the legs.</li></ul><h2>Important tips before giving the back injection in a cesarean delivery:</h2><ul><li>Follow Dr. Ahmed Marzouk''s instructions regarding the times to stop eating and drinking.</li><li>The injection should be given by an experienced anesthesiologist.</li></ul><p>When is the back injection taken for delivery? The back injection is taken at the onset of labor pains, or after the cervix has dilated by 3 cm.</p><h2>What are the contraindications for using the back injection in a cesarean delivery?</h2><ul><li>The woman''s refusal of the painless delivery injection.</li><li>The woman suffering from a bleeding (blood-thinning) disorder, increased brain pressure, brain tumors, or problems with the back vertebrae.</li><li>Inflammation or a skin infection at the site of the back injection.</li><li>In cases of previous blood loss due to hemorrhage.</li><li>Suffering from low blood pressure.</li><li>Having an allergy to any of the injection''s components.</li><li>In cases suffering from preeclampsia.</li></ul><p>Painless injection for cesarean delivery — its benefits and harms? The back injection offers many advantages, including:</p><ul><li>Avoiding the risks of general anesthesia in cesarean operations, which poses a danger in some cases such as heart and respiratory system diseases.</li><li>No need for artificial ventilation.</li><li>It limits pain better than the painkillers given in the case of general anesthesia.</li><li>The woman regains bowel movement and the ability to eat and drink faster and better than with general anesthesia.</li><li>It reduces the amount of anesthetic drugs reaching the fetus compared with general anesthesia.</li><li>It increases the mother''s bond with the newborn, as she stays awake and experiences all the stages of delivery.</li></ul><p>Despite the benefits of the back injection in cesarean operations, it does have some uncommon harms, and if they occur they quickly disappear, including: Low blood pressure: a few women may experience low blood pressure, so blood pressure is monitored during the operation so that it does not affect the continuity of blood flow to the fetus. Itching: some women may experience itching or minor skin irritation as a result of the anesthetic drugs, and to overcome this the doctor gives them anti-itching medications. Headache: some women may experience a headache after the back injection, so the doctor prescribes headache medications and advises drinking caffeine in moderation and consuming plenty of water. Elevated temperature: some women may experience a rise in temperature. Back pain: some women may experience back pain at the site of the painless delivery injection. Numbness in the legs: the doctor advises walking to relieve the numbness.</p><p>Price of the back injection for delivery: The price of the back injection for natural birth as well as for cesarean delivery varies due to several factors, including the doctor''s experience in cesarean operations and the anesthesiologist''s experience, as well as the experience of the doctor''s assisting team, the quality of the hospital where the operation will take place, the medications used, and the follow-up after the operation.</p><p>My experience with the back injection for cesarean delivery: One woman tells her experience of a painless cesarean delivery with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk using the back injection, where she was afraid of feeling pain when the injection was inserted into the back, which did not happen with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, and she did not feel any pain during the operation at all. She advises any woman who wants a painless cesarean delivery to go to Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, whom she considers the best in obstetric and gynecological surgeries.</p>' WHERE `id`='e178550c-d7c4-50cd-89c9-3655b4448a45';
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='blocked-fallopian-tubes', `title_en`='Blocked Fallopian Tubes: Causes and Treatment', `meta_title_en`='Blocked Fallopian Tubes | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='Learn the symptoms, causes, and treatment of blocked fallopian tubes, and your chances of pregnancy after treatment, with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, OB-GYN consultant.', `excerpt_en`='Blocked fallopian tubes directly affect fertility by preventing the egg from meeting the sperm. Learn the symptoms, causes, and treatment of blocked tubes, and your chances of pregnancy afterward, with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk.', `tag_en`='Fertility', `image_alt_en`='Diagram of blocked fallopian tubes affecting fertility — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>The fallopian tubes are the link that connects the ovary to the uterus, and they work to deliver the eggs produced by the ovary to the uterus each month so that they can be fertilized and thus form a fetus. The problem of blockage of these tubes is a major problem for some women because it hinders the occurrence of pregnancy. Learn with us about the symptoms of blocked fallopian tubes and how to treat them through my experience with a blocked fallopian tube with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, obstetrics and gynecology consultant, in this article.</p><h2>Symptoms that appear with blocked fallopian tubes:</h2><p>Usually the signs of a blocked fallopian tube do not appear right at the onset of the blockage, and a woman with blocked fallopian tubes does not know she has this condition until she faces a problem conceiving. However, some mild symptoms may appear in some women that help in diagnosing blocked fallopian tubes, such as the following:</p><ul><li>Regular pain on one side of the abdomen.</li><li>Severe pelvic pain associated with heavy menstrual bleeding.</li></ul><p>Blocked fallopian tubes are confirmed through a dye X-ray (hysterosalpingography) of the uterus and tubes in the first half of the menstrual cycle. An X-ray may also be taken of them, as well as the use of a medical laparoscope to confirm the diagnosis if the previous steps are not successful.</p><h2>Can pregnancy occur with blocked fallopian tubes? Or are the chances of pregnancy impossible?</h2><p>There is no doubt that blocked fallopian tubes directly affect fertility, because, as we mentioned, the blockage of the tubes prevents the eggs from meeting the sperm needed to fertilize them. The answer to the question of pregnancy and blockage depends on the degree of the blockage itself, whether it is a complete blockage or a partial blockage. In the case of a complete blockage of the fallopian tubes, pregnancy is impossible because the egg is unable to meet the sperm at all. In the case of a partial blockage, the chance of pregnancy is possible, but a woman with a partial blockage of the fallopian tubes may develop an ectopic pregnancy because of the difficulty of the fertilized eggs moving to the uterus through that blocked tube. Blocked fallopian tubes are divided into the following: blockage of one fallopian tube, which does not affect fertility completely; rather, with it the chances of pregnancy remain possible, but with an increased risk of an ectopic pregnancy, since the unfertilized egg may move freely within the one tube so that it can be fertilized, and the fertilized egg may move within the other blocked tube and an ectopic pregnancy may occur through it.</p><p>Blockage of both fallopian tubes: in this case pregnancy is directly affected, and no fertilization occurs at all because the egg is unable to reach the sperm needed to fertilize it. In this case, it is recommended to resort to intracytoplasmic sperm injection (ICSI) to increase the chance of achieving pregnancy.</p><h2>Causes of a blocked fallopian tube:</h2><h2>Blocked fallopian tubes have many causes, including the following:</h2><ul><li>Having endometriosis.</li><li>A previous ectopic pregnancy.</li><li>Previous surgery in the lower abdomen.</li><li>Having some uterine fibroids that must be removed with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk.</li><li>Having a severe inflammation in the pelvic area.</li><li>Contracting some sexually transmitted diseases such as chlamydia and gonorrhea.</li></ul><h2>Can blocked fallopian tubes be treated and pregnancy occur?</h2><p>The treatment of blocked fallopian tubes and the possibility of pregnancy depend on the type of blockage itself and its location, as we mentioned. The best treatment for a blocked fallopian tube with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk includes the following:</p><h3>Treating blocked fallopian tubes with medication:</h3><p>This is in the case where the main cause of the blockage is a sexually transmitted infection such as chlamydia or gonorrhea, and it is treated by taking oral or intravenous antibiotics.</p><h3>Treating blocked fallopian tubes with laparoscopy:</h3><p>This step is resorted to if the cause of the blocked fallopian tubes includes the other causes we mentioned, apart from a sexually transmitted infection. Through this step, any tissue growths or scars causing the blockage of the tubes are removed, as well as draining any fluids collected in them, in order to prevent them from leaking into the uterus and thus causing an infection in it.</p><h3>Treating blocked fallopian tubes with surgery:</h3><p>This is an advanced step for treating blocked fallopian tubes, and it is done in the case of the failure of the laparoscopy step to treat the blockage, when the blockage is severe and the damaged part of the tissue in the fallopian tubes cannot be removed through laparoscopy.</p><h2>Can I conceive after opening the fallopian tube?</h2><p>Yes, you can become pregnant after opening the blockage of your fallopian tubes, as the chances of pregnancy increase after treating the blockage of the fallopian tubes, especially if the blockage is close to the uterus and has been treated.</p><h2>What are the success rates of pregnancy after treating a blocked fallopian tube?</h2><p>The success rates of pregnancy after treating a blocked fallopian tube depend on the severity of the blockage itself, whether it is due to a lot of tissue or just some simple tissue, as well as on the location of the blockage itself, as the success rates of pregnancy decrease with a blockage close to the ovary and increase with a blockage close to the uterus. In general, you can consult Dr. Ahmed Marzouk to be sure of your chances of a successful pregnancy after treating blocked fallopian tubes, as each case differs from another in its severity and in how it is treated with him.</p>' WHERE `id`='67475b9c-0978-53c7-9571-49473ecb9f05';
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='vaginal-infection-treatment', `title_en`='Vaginal Infection Treatment: Types and Causes', `meta_title_en`='Vaginitis Treatment | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='Learn how to treat vaginal infections by type, their symptoms and causes, prevention tips, and treatment during pregnancy, with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, OB-GYN.', `excerpt_en`='Vaginal infections are common and vary in symptoms like itching and burning. Learn how to treat bacterial, fungal, and atrophic vaginitis, plus their causes, prevention, and management during pregnancy, with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk.', `tag_en`='Vaginal Health', `image_alt_en`='Woman consulting a doctor about vaginal infection treatment — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>Vaginal infections are widespread among a large number of women and differ in their symptoms from one woman to another, differing in symptoms such as itching and burning, and in the harms they cause, such as delayed pregnancy and health complications for a pregnant woman and her fetus. Therefore, attention must be paid to treating vaginal infections and to raising awareness of how to avoid them. Follow the article below with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk to learn more.</p><h2>The treatment of vaginitis differs according to the causes of the inflammation itself, and whether the inflammation occurred due to bacteria, fungi, or as a result of wrong habits:</h2><h3>1. Bacterial vaginitis:</h3><p>A woman can get a bacterial infection in the vaginal area as a result of a disturbance in the balance of the beneficial bacteria present in the vaginal area, due to a change in some hormones or contracting this infection from her husband. Bacterial vaginitis is characterized by the following symptoms:</p><ul><li>Gray-colored discharge.</li><li>Redness of the vagina.</li><li>A burning sensation in the vagina.</li><li>Pain when urinating.</li></ul><p>Bacterial vaginitis is treated by taking oral medications, such as metronidazole tablets (Flagyl) or clindamycin, and topical medications, such as clindamycin cream applied to the vagina or metronidazole gel (MetroGel), or by prescribing capsules placed inside the vagina.</p><h3>2. Fungal (yeast) vaginitis:</h3><p>It occurs as a result of a change in the acidity (pH) of the vaginal area due to hormonal changes, and the chances of it occurring increase as a result of frequent use of antibiotics or having diabetes. You can recognize its symptoms, which occur through:</p><ul><li>Itching in the vagina.</li><li>The appearance of thick white discharge resembling cheese.</li><li>A burning sensation in the vagina.</li><li>Pain during intercourse.</li></ul><p>Fungal vaginitis is treated with some oral medications such as fluconazole tablets, or clotrimazole or miconazole cream.</p><h3>3. Trichomoniasis:</h3><p>It results from a woman being infected with a type of parasite, and it is transmitted through sexual intercourse. Its most important symptoms are:</p><ul><li>Yellow or green, very foul-smelling discharge.</li><li>Severe itching.</li><li>Burning when urinating.</li><li>Feeling pain during intercourse.</li></ul><p>Trichomoniasis is treated by taking metronidazole tablets (Flagyl).</p><h3>4. Atrophic vaginitis:</h3><p>It is a type of vaginitis that occurs after menopause as a result of low estrogen levels, and atrophic vaginitis can be treated by taking medications that contain replacement estrogen hormone.</p><p>Given the problems that vaginitis causes for women, especially pregnant women, we will review with you the most important causes and risk factors that increase your chance of getting vaginitis.</p><h2>Causes of a vaginal infection:</h2><p>There are many causes of vaginitis, such as the following:</p><ul><li>A disruption of the proper balance of bacteria inside the vagina.</li><li>External contamination, whether bacterial or fungal.</li><li>Low estrogen levels during menopause.</li><li>Sexually transmitted diseases.</li><li>Frequent use of antibiotics.</li><li>Having diabetes.</li><li>Hormonal changes, especially during pregnancy.</li><li>Using contraceptive methods such as the IUD.</li><li>Heavy menstrual bleeding.</li></ul><h2>If you have vaginitis, how will you be diagnosed?</h2><p>1. Through knowing the patient''s medical history and whether you have previously suffered from any sexually transmitted diseases. 2. Through a pelvic examination and determining the acidity (pH) of the vagina: a vaginal examination helps to identify the causes of the discharge, and high acidity indicates the possibility of having trichomoniasis or a bacterial infection. 3. Taking a sample of the vaginal discharge: analyzing the vaginal discharge helps to reveal its cause and treat it.</p><h2>How do you protect yourself from getting vaginitis?</h2><p>1. Always wipe from front to back after going to the bathroom. 2. Refrain from washing the vagina with soap and use a special wash or water only. 3. Do not use vaginal douching, because it changes the balance of the germs naturally present in the vagina. 4. Use a condom during marital intercourse. 5. Wash your hands well and regularly. 6. Quit smoking. 7. Wear cotton underwear and stay away from tight trousers. 8. Change underwear more than once a day. 9. Do not use scented sanitary pads. 10. Do not use unsanitized swimming pools. 11. Eat yogurt and curdled milk for their role in maintaining the bacterial balance of the vagina. 12. Control the blood sugar level.</p><h2>Can you have intimate relations with your partner if you have vaginitis?</h2><p>If you have active vaginitis and feel the severity of the symptoms, then having intimate relations may cause you discomfort and may increase the severity of the symptoms. If the vaginitis is caused by trichomoniasis, which is sexually transmitted, you may transmit the infection to your partner. However, if the doctor has confirmed to you that you have a chronic fungal infection and you do not have severe and bothersome symptoms, then you can have intimate relations without worrying about the symptoms worsening, as long as it does not bother you when doing so, while paying attention to personal hygiene after intercourse and using your special antiseptic wash.</p><h2>Vaginitis and pregnancy:</h2><p>Vaginitis becomes active in a pregnant woman as a result of the major change in the body''s hormones, which causes her discomfort and itching. But the effect of having vaginitis does not stop there, as it causes many harms if left untreated, such as:</p><ul><li>The possibility of premature birth.</li><li>The possibility of miscarriage.</li><li>Low birth weight of the newborn.</li><li>Inflammation of the placenta.</li><li>Inflammation of the uterine lining after a cesarean delivery.</li><li>Death of the fetus.</li><li>Not treating vaginitis negatively affects a woman who wishes to become pregnant, because it causes a change in the acidity of the vagina, which affects the sperm and the possibility of fertilization occurring.</li></ul><h2>Treating vaginitis in a pregnant woman:</h2><p>In the case of a pregnant woman having vaginitis, the doctor prefers to use washes and topical treatments such as creams and vaginal suppositories, to avoid the effect of some oral medications on the fetus, especially in the first period of pregnancy.</p>' WHERE `id`='c8212000-f67e-5e44-a6f9-d1c4df08b9d0';
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='common-questions-after-cesarean', `title_en`='Common Questions After a Cesarean Delivery', `meta_title_en`='After a Cesarean: FAQs | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='Answers to common questions after a cesarean delivery: postpartum bleeding, recovery symptoms, showering, and intimacy, with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, OB-GYN.', `excerpt_en`='Going to have a cesarean? Get clear answers to common questions after a cesarean delivery, covering postpartum bleeding, recovery symptoms, showering, and intimacy, with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, OB-GYN specialist.', `tag_en`='Cesarean', `image_alt_en`='New mother recovering and asking questions after a cesarean delivery — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>Some women who are going to have a cesarean delivery may need answers to a number of questions that may come to mind. Follow along with us in this article titled "Common Questions After a Cesarean Delivery" with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, obstetrics and gynecology specialist.</p><h2>Reasons for the absence of postpartum bleeding after a cesarean delivery</h2><p>During a cesarean delivery, some incisions are made in the tissue of the uterus, and this cutting leads to the destruction of the blood vessels in the area adjacent to the uterus, and thus reduces the bleeding after delivery. In some cases, women who deliver by cesarean may experience less bleeding after the cesarean delivery compared with natural birth, and some of them may experience bleeding after forty days from the cesarean delivery. The following factors may contribute to the absence of postpartum bleeding after a cesarean delivery: 1. The use of some medications: some medications may be used for anesthesia and pain control after the operation, and some of these medications may affect the regulation of postpartum blood after the operation. 2. Blood clotting: in some cases, blood may clot inside the uterus after the cesarean, which leads to a reduction in postpartum bleeding. 3. Insufficient expansion of the uterus: the uterus may not expand sufficiently after the cesarean, which contributes to reduced postpartum bleeding.</p><h2>Symptoms after a cesarean delivery</h2><p>You may feel some common symptoms after a cesarean delivery, including the following: 1. Pain in the surgical area: you may feel pain and swelling in the cesarean surgical area. The pain may last for several days and may require taking painkillers to reduce its severity. 2. Low energy and feeling tired: a cesarean delivery is a surgical operation that requires going through a recovery period until the body regains its general strength, so you may feel tired and low in energy during the first recovery period. 3. Difficulty moving: because of the surgery and the muscular incision of the abdomen, you may face difficulty moving and difficulty performing some daily activities after delivery, so you will need some time to regain the ability to move normally. 4. Swelling and pain in the breasts: if you are breastfeeding, you may feel swelling and pain in the breasts due to the engorgement of milk and its accumulation in the breast. Cold compresses can be used on the breast to relieve the symptoms. 5. Mood changes: you may experience mood changes after delivery, such as a constant feeling of sadness. 6. Difficulty urinating: you may face some difficulties urinating after the cesarean, due to the effect of the surgery on the muscles and nerves surrounding the abdominal area.</p><h2>When can a postpartum woman shower after a cesarean delivery?</h2><p>A postpartum woman can shower after a cesarean delivery after a specific period of time to allow the wound to heal and to avoid any potential complications, and it is recommended to follow the following guidelines: 1. Consult your treating doctor: before showering after a cesarean delivery, you should consult your treating doctor about the date on which you can shower. 2. Wait until the wound heals: the wound must heal well before you shower. This often takes about two to three weeks, but the time may differ from one case to another. 3. Use lukewarm water and a mild soap that does not contain chemicals when showering after a cesarean delivery, then dry the area well after showering. 4. Hot water should be avoided, as it can increase the swelling of the wound and cause extra pressure. 5. Make sure to take good care of the wounds and the stitching site according to the treating doctor''s instructions, as you may need to change the dressings and clean the wounds regularly.</p><h2>Pain during marital relations after a cesarean delivery</h2><p>When is it permissible for a man to have intercourse with his wife after a cesarean delivery? The couple may face pain in sexual relations after a cesarean delivery, as it needs time to recover from the surgical operation, and there may be hormonal and physical changes that affect sexual desire and comfort during intimate relations. This may include some of the following symptoms and challenges: 1. Pain: the wife may feel pain in the area of the surgical incision after the cesarean delivery, which needs time to recover, and the pain may be present during intimate relations. 2. Sexual desire: there may be a change in the sexual desire of the postpartum woman after the cesarean delivery, based on the hormonal changes that can affect sexual desire and sexual communication between the two partners. 3. Increased emotions and tension: there may be intense tension and emotions after delivery, which may affect the marital relationship in general and sexual desire in particular. 4. Women may have concerns about the change in the shape of their bodies after delivery, such as weight gain or sagging, and this can affect self-confidence and readiness for intimate relations.</p><p>Therefore, it is important to talk frankly with your partner about the negative feelings you are experiencing and to ask him for help in getting rid of them so that you can get through this period with the least amount of complications. You should also consult Dr. Ahmed Marzouk about some matters, such as: when does the menstrual cycle return after a cesarean delivery? How do I avoid gas after the cesarean operation? How big is the cesarean wound? How long does bleeding continue after a cesarean delivery? Or if you notice any hard mass in the abdomen after the cesarean delivery.</p>' WHERE `id`='1be32ae3-55b1-571a-8689-600f784c7dc9';
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='cesarean-section-preparation-guide', `title_en`='Cesarean Preparation: Complete Day-Before Guide', `meta_title_en`='Cesarean Preparation Guide | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='Preparing for a cesarean? Get Dr. Ahmed Marzouk''s complete day-before guide: fasting hours, required tests, lung injections, and tips to ease your worries.', `excerpt_en`='As your cesarean approaches, a little preparation eases the worry. This complete guide covers the essential tests, fasting hours, lung injections, and practical tips you need the day before your C-section, straight from Dr. Ahmed Marzouk.', `tag_en`='Cesarean', `image_alt_en`='Pregnant woman preparing for a scheduled cesarean section the day before surgery — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>As your scheduled cesarean approaches, along with the hope of becoming a mother, some worries about the operation may arise. Here are the most important preparations you will need before a cesarean, as well as the key instructions and precautions to know before the procedure, from Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, consultant in obstetrics and gynecology.</p><p>Tips the day before a cesarean: Here are some of the most important steps before a cesarean that can help you prepare the day before the operation: 1. Avoid food and drink: You should follow Dr. Ahmed Marzouk''s instructions regarding fasting before the operation. You will usually be asked to refrain from eating and drinking for at least 8 hours before surgery to avoid anesthesia complications, and to stick only to the foods he specifies for you before the cesarean. 2. Follow the doctor''s instructions about medications: Make sure to follow the doctor''s instructions about which medications you should take or avoid before the operation. You may be asked to stop taking certain medications before the procedure to reduce the risks they could cause during the surgical incision. 3. Sleep well and rest: Try to get enough sleep the night before the operation, and make sure to get plenty of rest and relaxation as much as possible.</p><p>Tips on the day of the cesarean: 1. A cesarean may have a psychological effect on some women, so try to remind yourself that Dr. Ahmed Marzouk''s medical team is professional and that you will be in safe hands, so there is no need for stress and anxiety. 2. Prepare for your hospital stay: You may be asked to stay in the hospital for a while before the operation, so make sure to pack your essential items early, such as personal clothing, medical documents, sleeping items, and other personal necessities. 3. You may need someone to accompany and help you during the operation and to support you afterward, so make sure a close person is available who can provide you with assistance.</p><p>Treating a cough before a cesarean: If you have a cough before your cesarean and want to treat it or relieve its symptoms, here are some tips that can help you: 1. Try to get enough rest and relaxation, as relaxation may help calm the respiratory system and reduce coughing. 2. Drink warm fluids: Keep drinking warm fluids such as water, warm tea, or soup, as this helps moisten your throat and ease the cough. 3. Try to avoid irritants that may increase coughing, such as smoke, air pollutants, and exhaust fumes. 4. If the cough persists and causes you discomfort or anxiety, you should consult Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, who will give you specific guidance or prescribe suitable medications to relieve the cough after ensuring they are safe to use and appropriate for your health condition.</p><p>The lung injection before a cesarean: This is a procedure taken in some critical medical cases to promote the growth of the lungs in a fetus whose lungs have not matured sufficiently. It is a special medical procedure carried out in exceptional circumstances according to Dr. Ahmed Marzouk''s directions, and the decision of whether the lung injection is necessary or not is determined by Dr. Ahmed Marzouk based on an assessment of your medical condition and an ultrasound examination.</p><p>Cortisone injections to complete the fetus''s lung development are usually used when there is a significant risk to the fetus''s health or when there is a threat to its life. The injection is given to the pregnant mother and is transferred to the fetus through the active blood circulation between mother and fetus. Cortisol is a hormone produced by the adrenal gland that plays a role in the development and maturation of the lungs.</p><p>How do I overcome the fear before a cesarean? Fear and anxiety before a cesarean are natural, but there are some ways that can help you get rid of them or reduce them: 1. Understand and learn more about the cesarean operation, its effect on you, how it is performed, and what to expect from it. Ask the doctors and nurses about the details, procedures, and possible risks; this will greatly help reduce your anxiety. 2. Try to get emotional support from your partner, family members, or friends, talk to them about your fears and worries, and ask them for support and reassurance. 3. Practice deep breathing and daily relaxation techniques. 4. Plan ahead for the details related to the cesarean, such as the things you need to bring to the hospital. 5. Try to create a calming and comfortable environment at home: do activities that bring you happiness and relaxation, such as reading or listening to soothing music.</p><p>Cleaning the abdomen before a cesarean: This is a procedure sometimes done to disinfect the abdominal area before surgery. It is usually done by cleaning the abdomen with an antiseptic solution, such as iodine solution or chlorhexidine solution. This helps reduce the risk of infections after surgery, and it is carried out by the medical team assisting you before delivery. Therefore, you should talk to your healthcare team for more information about the abdominal cleaning procedure and whether it will be applied in your particular case.</p><p>Tests before a cesarean: Before performing a cesarean, you may be required to undergo some medical tests to assess your health and ensure your safety and the safety of the fetus. The tests requested may vary depending on your health condition and the condition of your fetus, but in general these are some common tests that may be requested before a cesarean, including: 1. Complete blood count: The hemoglobin level is measured before the cesarean, along with the red blood cell, white blood cell, and platelet counts, which helps assess blood health and detect any factors that would affect blood clotting. 2. Liver function test: Liver enzyme and liver protein levels are measured to assess liver health and diagnose any potential problems. 3. Kidney function test: Creatinine and urea levels are measured to assess kidney health and detect any problems in their function. 4. Blood sugar level test: The blood sugar level is measured to check for any problems in controlling sugar levels, especially if you have a family history of diabetes. 5. Blood type and Rh factor test: The blood type and Rh factor are determined to identify the blood group and ensure there is no conflict in blood types between the mother and the fetus.</p><p>Number of fasting hours before a cesarean: You are usually asked to fast for 6 to 8 hours before a cesarean. Fasting before the operation aims to avoid the risks of belching and vomiting during surgery, as the anesthesia tube is inserted, which requires the stomach to be empty.</p><p>Nevertheless, you should ask Dr. Ahmed Marzouk to determine the exact fasting duration you should follow before the cesarean, as well as the rest of the guidance to fully prepare for the operation.</p>' WHERE `id`='53a11a69-3257-5f26-ae25-72a4d81716e4';
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='polycystic-ovary-syndrome-pcos', `title_en`='PCOS: Symptoms, Causes and Treatment', `meta_title_en`='PCOS Treatment and Symptoms | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='Discover the symptoms, causes, and treatment of PCOS (polycystic ovary syndrome). Dr. Ahmed Marzouk explains diagnosis, complications, and prevention tips.', `excerpt_en`='Polycystic ovary syndrome is common after puberty and stems from a hormonal imbalance. Learn to recognize its symptoms, understand its causes and complications, and discover how Dr. Ahmed Marzouk diagnoses, treats, and helps you prevent PCOS.', `tag_en`='PCOS', `image_alt_en`='Illustration of polycystic ovaries showing fluid-filled follicles in a woman with PCOS — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is a common condition among many women after puberty. It occurs due to a disturbance in the reproductive hormones a woman produces, causing an increase in the production of the male hormone (androgen), which affects the ovary''s function in producing eggs (the egg may not develop normally or may not be released from the ovary at all). This causes a disruption in a woman''s menstrual cycle. PCOS is defined as a condition in which thin, fluid-filled follicles appear on the ovary itself, giving it its cystic appearance.</p><h2>When might I suspect that I have PCOS?</h2><p>If you notice any of the following:</p><p>Excess body hair appears, facial hair becomes thicker and darker in color, and body hair growth increases beyond normal.</p><ul><li>Obesity, especially around the waist, with difficulty losing weight.</li><li>Menstrual irregularity, in the form of heavy periods, absent periods, or a long interval between cycles each month.</li><li>Appearance of acne.</li><li>Appearance of acanthosis nigricans (thick, dark skin on the neck and under the arms).</li><li>Pain in the pelvic area.</li><li>Irregular ovulation.</li><li>Infertility.</li><li>Increased insulin resistance.</li><li>Anxiety and sleep disturbance.</li><li>Depression.</li><li>Eating disorders.</li><li>Exhaustion and fatigue.</li><li>Hair loss.</li></ul><h2>What are the causes that lead to PCOS?</h2><h3>Genetic factors:</h3><p>Although there is no specific gene responsible for PCOS, there is a group of genes that play a role in the transmission of this condition. Studies have found that about 25% of women who suffer from this condition had mothers who were affected by it, and that nearly 30% of women affected by it also had sisters who were affected.</p><h3>Insulin resistance:</h3><p>This is a condition in which the body''s cells become less sensitive to insulin, so the body tries to compensate by secreting more insulin, which causes an increase in insulin in the body. This leads the ovary to secrete more androgen hormone (a group of male hormones) and the appearance of PCOS symptoms.</p><h3>Hormonal disorders:</h3><p>Many women with PCOS suffer from hormonal disorders, such as an increase in male hormones, especially elevated testosterone, elevated levels of both prolactin and luteinizing hormone, and a decrease in sex hormone-binding globulin. These disorders appear as a result of insufficiency in the ovary itself or a dysfunction in the glands responsible for secreting these hormones.</p><h3>Low-grade inflammation:</h3><p>This results from an increase in the white blood cells responsible for fighting infection, which leads to increased androgen secretion from the ovary.</p><h2>Who is most at risk of developing PCOS?</h2><ul><li>Those who suffer from obesity and insulin resistance.</li><li>Women who have a family history of the condition.</li><li>Patients with type 2 diabetes.</li><li>Women who suffer from pelvic inflammation.</li><li>Those with endometriosis.</li></ul><h2>Factors that help predict the presence of PCOS:</h2><ul><li>Knowing the patient''s medical history and the symptoms she experiences.</li><li>Conducting tests to check the levels of insulin, testosterone, prolactin, luteinizing hormone, and sex hormone-binding globulin.</li><li>Checking the body mass index, the presence of acanthosis nigricans, an increased rate of excess body hair, and high blood pressure.</li><li>Ultrasound imaging of the pelvic area to detect polycystic ovaries.</li></ul><h2>How can PCOS be treated with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk?</h2><p>The treatment of PCOS depends on a set of factors such as:</p><ul><li>The patient''s age.</li><li>The patient''s general health.</li><li>The severity of the symptoms.</li><li>The desire to become pregnant in the future.</li></ul><p>Treatment plans range from changing lifestyle at first, then taking medications, and surgical intervention:</p><p>Lifestyle change: You can change your lifestyle and commit to a healthy diet to reduce insulin resistance and lose weight, as well as practice some physical exercise.</p><p>Treatment with medications:</p><ul><li>By taking birth control pills: These are used when the patient does not wish to become pregnant, and they help control menstrual cycles and lower androgen levels.</li><li>By taking diabetes medications: These are used to reduce insulin resistance and lower androgen hormone levels.</li><li>Through ovulation-stimulating medications for those who wish to become pregnant, which work to increase the rate of ovulation.</li><li>Through medications to stop hair loss.</li><li>To treat depression and sleep disorders.</li><li>Removing excess hair using laser technology.</li></ul><p>Treatment using surgical intervention: This is a simple surgical procedure performed using a laparoscope, in which the ovary is cauterized (ovarian drilling) to treat fertility problems.</p><h2>Complications of PCOS:</h2><p>If PCOS is not treated, it increases the chances of developing the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Heart disease.</li><li>High blood pressure.</li><li>Type 2 diabetes.</li><li>High cholesterol.</li></ul><h2>Pregnancy and PCOS:</h2><p>A pregnant woman with obesity and PCOS becomes more susceptible to gestational diabetes and high blood pressure after pregnancy, so you should follow these tips:</p><ul><li>Maintaining a normal blood sugar level before pregnancy.</li><li>Having a healthy weight and a body mass index between 18.5 and 25 before pregnancy.</li><li>Limiting caffeine consumption.</li><li>Taking dietary supplements such as folic acid.</li></ul><h2>How do you protect yourself from developing PCOS?</h2><ul><li>Maintaining a healthy weight and a body mass index not exceeding 25.</li><li>Eating healthy meals that contain vegetables, fruits, and fiber, and avoiding starches such as white bread and white flour to prevent insulin resistance.</li><li>Avoiding stress.</li><li>Keeping your blood sugar level under control and taking diabetes medications regularly, especially if you are a diabetic patient.</li><li>Exercising regularly to maintain your healthy weight and improve your mental well-being.</li></ul>' WHERE `id`='7fc1a2bb-332b-52d5-af31-5ee435c390c9';
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='semen-analysis-test', `title_en`='Semen Analysis: Reading Male Fertility Results', `meta_title_en`='Semen Analysis Test | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='What is a semen analysis and how are the results read? Dr. Ahmed Marzouk explains normal sperm count, motility, morphology, and how to improve fertility.', `excerpt_en`='A semen analysis examines the volume, color, count, motility, and shape of sperm to assess male fertility. Learn how to prepare, how the results are read, and how Dr. Ahmed Marzouk treats abnormal findings.', `tag_en`='Male Fertility', `image_alt_en`='Laboratory technician examining a semen analysis sample under a microscope for male fertility — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>This is a test to examine a man''s fertility, in which the volume of the semen, its color, and the shape, number, and motility of the sperm are examined. A semen analysis is one of the most important tests a doctor requests from a man who has a fertility problem and wants to know the causes and treat them. A distinction must be made between semen and sperm, as many people think there is no difference between them, but this is not true. Semen is a fluid that contains sperm in addition to other nourishing substances, and the fact that you produce semen during intercourse does not mean that you have enough sperm.</p><p>When will the doctor request a semen analysis for a man?</p><ul><li>To identify the causes of delayed conception.</li><li>To assess the success of a male sterilization procedure (vasectomy or tying of the vas deferens).</li></ul><p>What are the methods of obtaining the sample? The methods of obtaining the sample differ depending on whether it is at the testing laboratory or at home:</p><ul><li>By masturbation in the room designated for taking the sample at the laboratory.</li><li>By having intercourse at home using a condom.</li><li>By having intercourse with withdrawal before ejaculation and ejaculating the sample into the sterile cup.</li></ul><p>Are there conditions that affect how a semen analysis is done? There are some requirements that must be met to ensure the success of the test, including:</p><ul><li>Abstaining from intercourse for at least two days before the test.</li><li>Not abstaining from intercourse for more than a week before the test, so as not to affect the ability to ejaculate and the sample volume.</li><li>Abstaining from smoking, as it causes sperm deformities and affects their motility.</li><li>Avoiding the use of creams and lubricants when trying to produce the sample.</li><li>Making sure the sample is ejaculated entirely into the sterile cup given to you by the laboratory technician, with no part falling outside the cup.</li><li>It is preferable to produce the sample at the laboratory, but if produced at home, it should not take more than 30 minutes to bring it to the laboratory to ensure the accuracy of the sperm analysis.</li><li>When the doctor requests another sample from you, it is preferable to wait two or three weeks.</li></ul><p>How are the results of a male fertility test read?</p><p>Semen color. The normal color of semen is a whitish-gray. If the color changes to yellow, it may indicate jaundice or infection, and if the color changes to red, it indicates the presence of blood in the semen.</p><p>Semen quantity (sample volume). The sample volume should not be less than 2 milliliters. If the sample volume is less than 1.4, it may indicate a fertility problem resulting from inflammation, a varicocele in the testicle, or a blockage in the ejaculatory duct.</p><p>Semen pH level. The normal result ranges between 7.2 and 8. A severe change in acidity affects sperm motility and may lead to their death.</p><p>Semen liquefaction time (viscosity). This is the time the sample takes to become less viscous, ranging between 20 and 30 minutes. If it exceeds this time, it indicates excessive viscosity in the semen, which affects fertility, and this results from smoking or unhealthy eating.</p><p>Sperm count. The normal sperm count is estimated at 20 million sperm per milliliter of the sample. If the count is lower, the man may face difficulty in conceiving.</p><p>Sperm motility. Sperm motility is one of the most important indicators of a man''s fertility, and motility is good when more than 50% of the sperm move properly.</p><p>Rate of sperm abnormalities. A sperm consists of three parts: an oval head, a body (midpiece), and a tail. Any change in the sperm''s shape, such as having two heads, a round head, or a split or short tail, is considered a deformity in the sperm''s shape. For a man to be able to conceive, about 70% of the sperm should have a normal shape.</p><p>White blood cell count. The sample should not contain white blood cells, because their presence indicates an infection or inflammation in the testicle.</p><p>Causes of abnormal semen analysis results: Abnormal semen analysis results indicate the following:</p><ul><li>A varicocele (varicose veins of the testicle).</li><li>Inflammation of the testicle.</li><li>A hormonal imbalance.</li><li>Effect on the semen due to exposure to radiation or chemicals.</li><li>Infectious diseases such as mumps, which shrinks the size of the testicle.</li><li>Genetic defects and hereditary diseases.</li></ul><p>If you have a problem in your sperm analysis result, how is it treated?</p><ul><li>Surgical intervention to treat a varicocele or a blockage of the vas deferens.</li><li>Medications to reduce viscosity.</li><li>Medications to treat the semen''s pH level.</li><li>Hormonal therapies.</li><li>Antibiotics to treat infection.</li></ul><p>Tips to improve semen quality:</p><ul><li>Maintaining a healthy diet that includes vegetables and fruits and avoiding fats and sugars.</li><li>Abstaining from smoking and alcohol and reducing caffeine.</li><li>Losing weight and exercising.</li><li>Getting enough sleep and avoiding stress.</li></ul><p>Finally, now that we have learned what a semen analysis is and how it is performed, if you want to reassure yourself about your fertility or if you face any problem related to delayed conception after having a semen analysis, regardless of the cost of a semen analysis, you can turn to Dr. Ahmed Marzouk''s center, which has extensive experience in treating delayed conception and fertility problems.</p>' WHERE `id`='4ee5a60f-d75b-53c8-935c-1765073df51a';
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='tummy-tuck-after-cesarean', `title_en`='Tummy Tuck After a C-Section: My Experience', `meta_title_en`='Tummy Tuck After C-Section | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='Why does the belly stay big after a cesarean? Dr. Ahmed Marzouk shares tips on tummy tuck after a C-section, weight loss, and restoring your abdomen''s shape.', `excerpt_en`='A saggy belly after a cesarean bothers many mothers. Discover why the abdomen enlarges, how long it takes to recover, and the diet, exercise, and tummy-tuck tips Dr. Ahmed Marzouk recommends to restore its shape.', `tag_en`='Postpartum', `image_alt_en`='Woman checking her abdomen after a cesarean section considering a tummy tuck — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>A saggy belly after a cesarean is bothersome to many of us, so is there a solution for it? Yes, the solution lies in a tummy tuck after a cesarean, which greatly improves the shape of the abdomen after a cesarean. Let me tell you some things related to my experience with a tummy tuck after a cesarean.</p><p>Causes of an enlarged abdomen after a cesarean: An enlarged abdomen after a cesarean can have several causes that may contribute to the increase in abdominal size, including the following: 1. Fat accumulation: During pregnancy, fat is stored in the abdominal area to support the growth and nutrition of the fetus, and after delivery, fat may continue to accumulate in this area and lead to an increase in abdominal size. 2. Intestinal bloating: A cesarean is performed through a surgical procedure on the abdomen, which causes bloating and swelling in the abdominal area after delivery. 3. Stretching of muscles and tissues: The uterus, muscles, and tissues in the abdominal area stretch to accommodate the growth of the fetus during pregnancy, and after delivery these muscles and tissues may remain stretched, contributing to the increase in abdominal size. 4. Reduced muscle strength of the abdominal muscles: The strength and fitness of the abdominal muscles may be affected by pregnancy and delivery, leading to weakness and bulging in the abdominal area. 5. Skin sagging: The skin may sag after delivery, especially if there was an increase in abdominal size and significant skin stretching during pregnancy.</p><p>Taking into account that these causes may be temporary and fade over time with good care of the abdomen, following a healthy diet, and doing appropriate exercise after delivery.</p><p>How do I take care of my abdomen after a cesarean? Here are some tips that will help you maintain the health and shape of your abdomen after a cesarean: 1. You should follow the treating doctor''s instructions carefully, especially the instructions for cleaning the wound and changing the dressings regularly. 2. Rest and relaxation: Make sure to get enough rest and relaxation. Listen to your body and do not rush into strenuous activities until the wound has healed and the body has fully recovered. 3. Eat healthy meals: Maintain a healthy, balanced diet. Eat nutritious meals containing proteins, fiber, fruits, and vegetables, avoid processed and fatty foods, and be sure to drink enough water. 4. It may be helpful to start exercises to strengthen the core abdominal muscles after an appropriate amount of time has passed following the recovery period, such as core muscle exercises, crunches, and oblique muscle exercises, as they can directly help strengthen the abdominal muscles. 5. Maintain a suitable weight: Maintaining a healthy body weight can help preserve the shape of the abdomen, as losing excess weight may help reduce sagging and improve the appearance of the abdomen, along with wearing an abdominal binder after a cesarean after taking your own doctor''s advice. 6. Do not hesitate to seek emotional support from your partner, family members, or friends, as it can be beneficial for maintaining your overall health.</p><p>When does the abdomen return to normal after a cesarean? After a cesarean, the abdominal muscles and the surrounding tissues stretch and fat accumulates in this area, so the return of the abdomen to its normal size after a cesarean may take some time, and this time depends on several factors, including the following: 1. The body''s health condition: A woman''s general health affects the recovery of the abdomen after delivery, especially in cases of excess weight, chronic health problems, and unbalanced hormone levels. 2. Genetic factors: Genetic influence plays a role in the return of the abdomen after delivery. Some women may find that they regain their previous abdominal shape quickly, while others may need a longer time. 3. Physical activity: Doing appropriate exercise and strengthening the core muscles can help restore the strength and shape of the abdomen. Regular, gradual physical activity such as walking and strengthening exercises can help promote muscle toning and reduce fat accumulation.</p><p>Bearing in mind that although some women may notice an improvement in the shape of the abdomen a few weeks after a cesarean, it may take several months for the abdomen to return to its normal size in other women. Therefore, it is important to take enough time to recover after a cesarean and to take care of yourself and your health as much as possible.</p><p>When does weight loss begin after a cesarean? The time weight loss begins can vary from one woman to another after a cesarean. Here are some factors that control the start of weight loss after a cesarean: 1. Fluid loss: After the operation, women may notice a drop in weight due to the loss of excess fluids in the first days after delivery. 2. Uterine contraction: The uterus begins to contract after delivery, and this helps reduce the size of the uterus and return the abdomen to its normal size. The duration of uterine contraction varies from one woman to another, and uterine contraction may lead to loss of excess weight. 3. Breastfeeding: If you plan to breastfeed, this process may help you lose weight faster, since during breastfeeding extra calories are burned while producing milk and nursing the baby. 4. Healthy nutrition: Attention should be paid to healthy nutrition after a cesarean, and eating balanced meals rich in important nutrients such as protein, fiber, fruits, and vegetables can help maintain a healthy weight and achieve loss of excess weight. 5. Doing exercise such as walking can help burn calories and promote weight loss.</p><p>Nevertheless, you should take into account that every woman is unique and her recovery after a cesarean differs from one woman to another. Therefore, it is recommended to talk to the treating doctor to get appropriate guidance on how to deal with a saggy belly after a cesarean and how to try to reduce that sagging as much as possible.</p>' WHERE `id`='7ec779f5-0d2d-51c9-b18b-f68ea96c6922';
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='pregnancy-after-cesarean', `title_en`='Pregnancy After a C-Section: What to Know', `meta_title_en`='Pregnancy After a C-Section | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='Can you give birth naturally after a cesarean? Dr. Ahmed Marzouk answers questions on pregnancy after a C-section, contraception, and your period''s return.', `excerpt_en`='Is pregnancy possible after a cesarean, and can you deliver naturally next time? Dr. Ahmed Marzouk answers common questions about contraception, tubal ligation, vaginal birth after cesarean, and when your period returns.', `tag_en`='Cesarean', `image_alt_en`='Pregnant woman with a previous cesarean scar consulting about pregnancy after a C-section — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>Pregnancy after a cesarean is a source of concern for many women who have previously undergone a cesarean delivery: can they give birth naturally afterward, or has a cesarean become a permanent commitment? So let us address some of the questions that may come to mind about the relationship between natural birth and cesarean delivery and answer them with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk in the following article.</p><p>When do I take birth control pills after a cesarean? Starting to use birth control pills after a cesarean depends on some important points that must be considered, which are as follows: 1. It is preferable to wait until the wound has healed and the body has fully recovered after the cesarean before starting to use birth control pills, which may require waiting about 4 to 6 weeks after the cesarean, while bearing in mind that this period differs from one case to another. 2. The time at which you can start birth control pills also depends on the type of pills you wish to use, as some types of birth control pills contain certain hormones and require a certain amount of time before they become fully effective. 3. The treating doctor''s guidance, as the doctor will assess your health condition and whether the recovery period has ended or not, then provide you with appropriate guidance based on that. 4. Other contraceptive options that may suit you should also be discussed with the doctor, as other contraceptive methods such as condoms, a hormonal IUD, or others may suit you.</p><p>Tubal ligation after a cesarean: Tubal ligation after a cesarean can be defined as a surgical procedure used to prevent pregnancy permanently by tying the two fallopian tubes or rearranging them so that eggs are prevented from reaching the uterus and mixing with sperm.</p><p>Tubal ligation is a surgical procedure that prevents pregnancy from occurring again, and it is usually resorted to by couples who do not wish to have more children. Tubal ligation is usually performed during a cesarean, but the treating doctor should be consulted to discuss the expectations and risks associated with tubal ligation and to request the necessary guidance to make a correct decision that will not be regretted later.</p><p>Is a natural birth possible after a cesarean? Facilitating a natural birth after a cesarean depends on many factors, including: 1. The reason for the previous cesarean: Facilitating a natural birth after a cesarean depends on the reason for the previous cesarean, as in some cases there may be medical or surgical factors that make a natural birth undesirable. 2. The type of the previous cesarean: There are two main types of cesarean: a cesarean based on emergency medical reasons and an elective cesarean. An emergency cesarean can cause serious complications and may increase the likelihood of needing a cesarean in any subsequent pregnancy, but if the previous cesarean was elective and there are no other health complications, you may have a good chance of a natural birth. 3. The treating doctor should assess the current condition of the pelvis and uterus and determine whether there are any factors that make a natural birth unsafe or difficult. 4. It is important to have continuous medical monitoring during a natural birth after a cesarean in order to control any complications or problems that may occur, as there may be potential risks associated with attempting a natural birth after a cesarean, such as a rupture in the upper region of the uterus (uterine rupture), which can be very dangerous. Therefore, these risks and potential benefits should be discussed with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk at the outset before making a decision about a natural birth after a cesarean, based on what you experience with a natural birth after a cesarean.</p><p>When does menstruation return after a cesarean? The return of menstruation after a cesarean can vary from one woman to another, but there are several factors that may affect the timing of the return of menstruation after a cesarean in each woman, including the following: 1. Breastfeeding: If you are breastfeeding, this may affect the return of menstruation. Breastfeeding can delay the return of menstruation, especially if you are nursing throughout the day and night. However, breastfeeding cannot be relied upon as an effective method of contraception, and the doctor should be consulted about safe and suitable contraceptive methods. 2. Nutrition and weight: Healthy nutrition and maintaining a suitable weight may affect the return of menstruation. Menstruation usually returns after the body regains its normal weight. 3. Hormonal factors: A change may occur in the body''s hormone levels after delivery, and this may significantly affect the return of menstruation.</p><p>In general, menstruation can return after a cesarean within 6 to 8 weeks of delivery, so if your period is delayed, we advise you to go immediately to Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, obstetrics and gynecology specialist, for an examination and to determine the cause and treat it promptly.</p>' WHERE `id`='f0837ae0-7862-5f21-b3ad-5f8f768b5ea5';
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='uterine-fibroids-and-pregnancy', `title_en`='Uterine Fibroids and Pregnancy: Key Facts', `meta_title_en`='Uterine Fibroids & Pregnancy | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='Uterine fibroids and pregnancy: discover the symptoms, bleeding risks, and your real chances of conceiving after fibroid removal with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk.', `excerpt_en`='Uterine fibroids are increasingly common and raise worries about conception. Learn whether fibroids prevent pregnancy, how they behave during pregnancy, and the chances of conceiving after removal, with expert insight from Dr. Ahmed Marzouk.', `tag_en`='Fibroids', `image_alt_en`='Illustration of uterine fibroids and their effect on pregnancy — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>Uterine fibroids have become widespread among many women recently, and with them concern has grown over delayed pregnancy and conception, and over the nature of the relationship between fibroids and pregnancy: does a fibroid prevent pregnancy or not? All these answers are provided with great skill and years of experience by Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, specialist in obstetrics and gynecological surgery, in this article.</p><p>Symptoms of fibroids during pregnancy: Fibroids are benign tumors that grow in the muscles of the uterus, and they are often asymptomatic or cause only mild symptoms in most cases. However, some women may notice mild symptom changes during pregnancy due to the hormonal changes it brings about and the expansion of the uterus. Here are some symptoms that may appear in women who have fibroids during pregnancy: 1. Pelvic pain: Some women may feel mild pain or pressure in the pelvic area due to the expansion of the uterus and the increase in the size of the fibroids. 2. Increased pressure on the bladder: Fibroids may press on the bladder, leading to increased urination or a frequent urge to urinate. 3. Increased constipation: Fibroids can press on the intestines in some cases, causing constipation. 4. Lower back pain: Some women with fibroids may experience lower back pain.</p><p>Do fibroids grow with pregnancy? Yes, an increase in the size of fibroids may occur during pregnancy in some cases, due to the hormonal changes that take place during pregnancy which contribute to the expansion of the uterus; this expansion can therefore lead to an increase in the size of the fibroids present in the uterus.</p><p>The size of fibroids may not change during pregnancy, or a very slight change may occur in the uterus, depending on several factors including the size of the fibroid, its location, its effect on the uterus, and the hormonal changes taking place.</p><p>Fibroids outside the uterus during pregnancy: These are considered a rare type of fibroid, as they grow in the pelvic area outside the uterus itself. However, if a fibroid outside the uterus is discovered during pregnancy, you should take the following into account: 1. A woman who has a fibroid outside the uterus during pregnancy should visit the obstetrician and gynecologist regularly, to monitor the size of the fibroid and assess its effect on the pregnancy and the surrounding organs. 2. Symptoms occurring during pregnancy, such as pain or bleeding, should be monitored, and a careful medical evaluation undergone in an attempt to control any possible complications, such as fetal deformities or the fibroid negatively affecting the delivery process and posing a danger to the fetus and mother. 3. At times, a decision may need to be made to resort to surgery to remove the fibroid during or after pregnancy, based on Dr. Ahmed Marzouk''s opinion, the progress of the pregnancy, the size of the fibroid, and the resulting symptoms; therefore it is essential that you stay in constant contact with us and follow up on the progress of the pregnancy throughout, if you have a fibroid outside the uterus.</p><p>Do fibroids cause bleeding during pregnancy? Yes, a fibroid may cause bleeding while it is present in the uterus during pregnancy, and the amount of bleeding that occurs depends on the size of the fibroid, its location, and how it affects the surrounding tissues. If the fibroid is large or if its location is close to the inner uterine wall, it can cause irritation of the surrounding tissues and thus increase the likelihood of bleeding during pregnancy.</p><p>Bleeding from a fibroid during pregnancy may vary in severity from one woman to another, and may be accompanied by pain or abdominal cramps; therefore, if you feel any bleeding during pregnancy or are worried about any symptoms, you should contact us immediately for an accurate and rapid assessment of your health and the condition of your fetus.</p><p>Pregnancy after fibroid removal: Does a fibroid affect pregnancy after its removal? Or does a fibroid prevent pregnancy after its removal? This question is one of the most important questions that may occupy the minds of many women, and we can answer it by saying that after removing the fibroid from the uterine wall or from inside the uterus itself, women can become pregnant naturally if their health allows it, and provided the fibroid has not affected any vital organ around the uterus or even the uterus itself. Now, here are some things you should take into account after fibroid removal to increase the chances of pregnancy: 1. The body needs time to heal after surgery, and you should take into account passing through the recovery period completely safely before trying to conceive. 2. It is essential to consult Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, consultant in obstetrics and gynecology, and to attend follow-up sessions regularly after surgery, to monitor the developments of your health and to know when pregnancy can be safely attempted. 3. You should take care of your health as much as possible, and commit to good nutrition and regular exercise, as they play an important role in improving overall health and thus increasing the chances of pregnancy. 4. Track your menstrual cycle and know your ovulation dates, as this can help determine the best time to try to conceive. 5. In some cases, the doctor may recommend taking some hormonal treatment to enhance the chances of pregnancy. 6. Follow up on the pregnancy when you become pregnant, and you must adhere to the doctor''s guidance to maintain the health of the fetus and yourself.</p><p>Pregnancy rate after fibroid removal: Determining the success rate of pregnancy after fibroid removal, and knowing whether a fibroid really prevents pregnancy or not, depends on several factors including the size of the fibroid, its location, and how it affects the surrounding organs. In many cases, pregnant women who have small fibroids can conceive and give birth naturally after surgical removal of the fibroid. Some women may also face difficulty conceiving after fibroid removal if the fibroids were very large or caused significant damage to the uterus or any of the vital organs around it. Therefore, you should consult Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, obstetrician and gynecologist, to assess the situation for your personal case, and to obtain accurate information about the possibility of pregnancy after surgical removal of the fibroid.</p><p>With Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, you can treat all uterine fibroids easily and comfortably, and you can expect that having a fibroid and pregnancy with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk is not impossible, for the doctor is a pioneer in treating many cases of fibroids and achieving the dream of motherhood for them after they had lost hope for many years.</p>' WHERE `id`='e9a0c89d-b332-5e2c-bace-567e34f1570f';
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='icsi-microinjection', `title_en`='ICSI: How Microinjection Works', `meta_title_en`='ICSI Microinjection | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='Discover what ICSI (microinjection) is, how the procedure works step by step, who needs it, its success rate, and possible risks with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk.', `excerpt_en`='ICSI (microinjection) helps many couples conceive by fertilizing the egg with a single sperm in the lab. Learn how the procedure works, who it suits, its success rate, and possible risks with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk.', `tag_en`='Fertility', `image_alt_en`='ICSI microinjection procedure fertilizing an egg with a single sperm — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>ICSI (intracytoplasmic sperm injection) procedures have become widespread recently, and we have begun to hear about many of our friends and relatives undergoing them to help them conceive. So what is ICSI? How is this procedure done? And how does it help them conceive?</p><h2>What Is ICSI and How Is It Performed?</h2><p>ICSI is used to fertilize the egg with a sperm in the laboratory, then implant it in the uterus or the fallopian tube. It is done through the following steps, which explain the method of a successful ICSI:</p><h3>Ovulation Stimulation:</h3><p>This is done by giving the wife medications that stimulate the ovary to produce eggs suitable for fertilization, and also work to increase certain hormones that help stimulate egg production. The eggs are monitored over two weeks before collecting the eggs that will be fertilized. In the first week, the doctor will test the estrogen hormone in the blood and, through ultrasound, perform a careful examination. Then, in the second week, he begins giving other medications that control the hormones which help stimulate the eggs to mature, so that he can extract a mature egg that he can fertilize outside the body.</p><h3>Egg Retrieval:</h3><p>In this step, after the doctor confirms that the eggs are suitable for fertilization and have become fully mature, he begins to collect these eggs within 24 to 36 hours at most, using a precise medical scope, either through the abdomen or through the vagina. They are then placed in the laboratory under certain conditions that preserve them and their fitness for fertilization.</p><h3>Sperm Collection:</h3><p>At this stage, the doctor will ask the husband to collect sperm through masturbation, or by making a small surgical incision in the testicle if the husband has any medical condition that prevents the growth and development of sperm, so that they become suitable for fertilizing the egg.</p><h3>Fertilization:</h3><p>This is the step that begins with preparing the semen for fertilization by subjecting it to centrifugation, so that healthy sperm are separated from dead ones. After that, the doctor begins selecting a single sperm suitable for the egg fertilization process, then begins injecting it directly into the fertilized egg taken from the wife. After confirming the egg''s fertilization, he moves to the next stage, which is the stage of preserving the fertilized egg.</p><h3>Preserving the Fertilized Egg:</h3><p>In this step of the ICSI steps, only the fertilized eggs that have been fertilized for three to five days are selected, then the fittest of them are implanted in the uterus through the cervix using a very thin tube designed for implantation. After that, the symptoms of pregnancy after ICSI begin to appear in the event of its success and the husband and wife following everything the treating physician tells them about what is permitted and prohibited after ICSI.</p><h2>When Can We Resort to ICSI?</h2><p>ICSI experiences are suitable for anyone who has a problem that hinders their ability to conceive, such as:</p><ul><li>Men who have a low sperm count.</li><li>Men who have poor sperm motility.</li><li>Men whose sperm are unable to penetrate the egg.</li><li>Men who have poor-quality sperm unsuitable for fertilization.</li><li>Women who have a thick wall around their eggs, making it difficult for the sperm to penetrate them.</li><li>Blockage problems in the sperm ducts in men.</li></ul><h2>What Are the Disadvantages of ICSI, and Is It Completely Safe?</h2><p>ICSI is not considered 100% safe; it may involve some complications after undergoing an ICSI procedure, like any laboratory intervention. It may also fail as a result of the absence of one of the conditions for ICSI success. Among the causes of ICSI failure are the following:</p><ul><li>Failure of the fertilized egg to grow and develop into a living embryo.</li><li>The embryo stopping its growth after the egg is fertilized.</li><li>Damage to the egg in the first place.</li><li>Continued stimulation of the ovary to produce eggs due to an increase in the amount of stimulants the wife has taken.</li><li>Increased chances of having babies with some congenital defects.</li><li>Increased chances of miscarriage.</li></ul><h2>Advantages You Will Gain After Undergoing ICSI:</h2><ul><li>It will increase your chances of conception.</li><li>It will increase ovarian activity, as some women may conceive naturally after a failed ICSI procedure.</li><li>ICSI is considered to have a somewhat high success rate.</li><li>ICSI can be repeated more than once and increase your chances of conception.</li></ul><h2>When Do the Signs of ICSI Success Appear, and Is ICSI Painful?</h2><p>The couple needs a period of between 4 to 5 weeks after the ICSI procedure to detect the symptoms of ICSI failure or success and to confirm the presence of an embryo, by performing a home pregnancy test. However, we advise going to the specialist doctor immediately to perform an ultrasound scan to confirm the presence of a pregnancy, along with giving some pregnancy-supporting medications that help increase your chance of keeping your fetus and reduce the chances of miscarriage. ICSI is also not painful in its steps at all, and if the wife feels some pain, we can control her feeling of pain by taking some simple painkillers under our medical supervision.</p><h2>ICSI Success Rate:</h2><p>ICSI is considered to have a high success rate compared to other conception procedures such as IVF or artificial insemination, as it has a success rate of approximately 80% among those who show signs of ICSI success. It is also considered the most famous procedure in the world for achieving the dream of motherhood, as approximately 8 million babies are born annually, especially after following the post-ICSI instructions their doctors give them. So there is no need to worry, dear.</p><p>The cost of ICSI: The prices of ICSI vary greatly depending on several factors, including the reputation and experience of the doctor with whom you will undergo the procedure. The price of ICSI is also affected by the differing costs of the medications you will need in the procedure, as well as the examinations you will undergo, and also by the problem for which you are resorting to ICSI, the price of the similar IVF procedure, and the price of ICSI at well-known medical centers.</p>' WHERE `id`='e86db0db-526c-50cd-83ba-8a92b8057677';
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='painless-cesarean-tap-block', `title_en`='Painless C-Section with TAP Block', `meta_title_en`='Painless C-Section: TAP Block | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='Learn how the TAP Block enables a painless cesarean, how it differs from PCA, its benefits, steps, and side effects, with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk in Egypt.', `excerpt_en`='The TAP Block lets mothers experience an almost painless cesarean section. Discover how this technique works, its benefits and side effects, and how it compares to the PCA device, with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk.', `tag_en`='Painless Delivery', `image_alt_en`='TAP Block injection for a painless cesarean section — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>A painless cesarean section is a dear wish for every mother who dreams of achieving the dream of motherhood and holding her newborn in her arms, but who fears the pain of childbirth. However, the women who chose Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, consultant in obstetric and gynecological surgery, have been able to achieve this wish, as Dr. Ahmed Marzouk uses the painless-delivery TAP Block injection to make the woman not feel the pain of the cesarean section.</p><p>What is painless delivery? It is delivery in which the surgeon tries to relieve the pain of opening the abdomen and the pain of the surgical incision after the delivery operation, through modern medical techniques. Among the most famous of these means that help you go through a painless delivery are the TAP Block device and the PCA device.</p><p>My experience with TAP Block at Dr. Ahmed Marzouk''s center: Iman recounts her experience saying: ''I never imagined that a cesarean could be this easy and almost without pain in the incision area. Dr. Ahmed is not only a skilled gynecologist, but also an excellent psychologist who cares about the patient''s psychological state, follows up on her, and checks on her after the operation. I am very happy with my experience of the painless cesarean section with him.''</p><p>What is the TAP Block painless cesarean injection? When a mother undergoes a cesarean section due to her health condition or because of the position of the fetus, she suffers from pain after delivery because of the incision in the lower abdomen once the effect of the anesthesia wears off. However, Dr. Ahmed Marzouk provides the TAP Block technique for painless delivery, a technique in which the abdominal wall is anesthetized via the TAP Block (Transversus Abdominis Plane Block), in which the nerve supplying the abdominal area where the incision is located is anesthetized, thereby stopping the pain signals coming from this area so that they do not reach the brain. This enables the woman to move after the operation without feeling the pain of the incision for up to 24 hours or more, and it also greatly reduces the feeling of incision pain for several days after the operation, as the TAP Block injection significantly relieves the pain of the incision.</p><p>The form of the TAP Block injection: The TAP Block needle is an ordinary needle containing a group of strong painkillers that stop the pain signals reaching the abdomen when given to the patient in the area of the abdominal muscles.</p><p>What are the steps followed before giving the TAP Block injection? The doctor explains to the mother how the TAP Block injection works and its benefits, and measures blood pressure and pulse. The injection site is sterilized, and the doctor makes sure there is no allergy to any of the injection''s components.</p><p>What is the TAP Block anesthesia method?</p><ul><li>The anesthesiologist anesthetizes the patient, either general anesthesia or anesthesia using an epidural injection, according to her health condition.</li><li>In the case of an epidural injection, the patient is conscious through the stages of delivery.</li><li>Dr. Ahmed Marzouk makes an incision in the lower abdomen above the pubis.</li><li>Then he cuts the abdominal muscles until he reaches the uterus.</li><li>The doctor delivers the baby and cuts the umbilical cord.</li><li>He removes the placenta from the uterus and closes it with dissolvable stitches.</li><li>He identifies the appropriate area for the TAP Block injection using an ultrasound device.</li><li>The TAP Block needle is directed perpendicularly between the abdominal muscles until the tip of the needle reaches the space above the transversus abdominis muscle.</li><li>He takes care that the needle is not inside a blood vessel.</li><li>He slowly injects the contents of the syringe, then withdraws the needle.</li></ul><p>What are the advantages of painless delivery using the TAP Block?</p><ul><li>The TAP Block injection relieves pain for a longer time after the cesarean section, as its effect of not feeling the surgical pain continues for up to 24 hours, and often extends to 36 hours.</li><li>There are no common side effects, since it does not contain the opioids that are part of the composition of other painkillers.</li></ul><p>Side effects of the TAP Block needle? The TAP Block needle can cause harm if injected incorrectly or in the wrong place, so it must be injected by a skilled and highly experienced anesthesiologist, which is what Dr. Ahmed Marzouk''s center provides.</p><p>The TAP Block injection also relieves only the pain of the incision resulting from the cesarean operation and does not relieve uterine pain.</p><p>What is PCA? It is one of the modern techniques in painless cesarean delivery, in which the woman receives a dose of strong painkillers such as morphine, fentanyl, and hydromorphone, by connecting the PCA device to the patient''s vein through a thin tube attached to a cannula fixed in the woman''s hand. After this, the woman is able to walk and move without pain, which reduces the chance of clots occurring after delivery. However, it can cause drowsiness and some stomach pain and constipation.</p><p>What is the difference between the TAP Block injection and the PCA? The TAP Block technique differs from the PCA, as the TAP Block is a local anesthetic injected into the abdominal area to relieve only the pain resulting from the incision, so the woman is able to move after the operation without feeling the incision pain for a period ranging between 24 hours and 36 hours. Meanwhile, the PCA device relieves both the incision pain and the uterine pain, as the PCA device contains a group of strong painkillers that are attached via a cannula to the mother''s vein after delivery, which release a group of painkillers in specific doses when the device''s button is pressed when the mother feels pain.</p><p>The doctor, in agreement with the patient, decides the choice between the TAP Block and the PCA according to the patient''s health condition, the extent of her sensation and tolerance of pain, whether she suffers from an allergy to one of the types of painkillers, the cost, and a comparison of the advantages and effects resulting from each of the two options.</p><p>What is the price of the TAP Block injection in Egypt? The price of a cesarean section using the TAP Block varies as a result of several factors, including the experience of the doctor and his assisting team, the experience of the anesthesiologist, the quality of the hospital in which the operation will take place, the medications used, and the follow-up after the operation.</p><p>If you wish to have a painless delivery, you can go to Dr. Ahmed Marzouk''s center, consultant in obstetric and gynecological surgery, and his assisting medical team, who have been trained to the highest level and on the latest techniques.</p>' WHERE `id`='941791c1-7956-51c9-ad73-a8ce1acf60d1';
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='risks-after-cesarean-section', `title_en`='Risks After a Cesarean Section', `meta_title_en`='Risks After a C-Section | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='Learn the common risks after a cesarean section, from breast engorgement and hemorrhoids to leg pain and menstrual changes, with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk.', `excerpt_en`='Worried about life after a cesarean section? Discover the most common post-C-section risks, including breast engorgement, menstrual changes, leg pain, and hemorrhoids, and how to manage them with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk.', `tag_en`='C-Section', `image_alt_en`='Common health risks a mother may face after a cesarean section — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>Many women fear childbirth, especially cesarean delivery, and many keep asking and searching for the most well-known risks after a cesarean section, and for the most well-known problems they may hear about that can occur after a cesarean. So rest assured, madam, for in this article, together with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, specialist in obstetrics and gynecology, we will take a close look at the most well-known risks after a cesarean section.</p><p>Treatment of breast engorgement after a cesarean section: Breast engorgement (mastitis) is an inflammation of the breast that occurs after a cesarean section during the period of breastfeeding the baby, resulting from the retention of breast milk in the breast, which may cause pain, redness, swelling, and heat in the affected area, and it may also be accompanied by a fever. If you suffer from breast engorgement after a cesarean section, here are some measures and tips that can help relieve the symptoms and speed up recovery: 1. Rest and good nutrition: Try to rest and eat balanced, nutrient-rich meals to strengthen the immune system and speed up the healing process. 2. Breastfeeding: If you are breastfeeding, try to continue breastfeeding regularly from the affected breast. Breastfeeding can help facilitate the drainage of milk and reduce retention. 3. Warm water compresses: Use warm water compresses to relieve pain and stimulate milk flow. Topical warming ointments can also be used. 4. Massage: Gently massage the breast while showering to help drain the milk and reduce swelling. 5. Antibiotics: If your inflammation is severe and accompanied by a fever, your doctor may prescribe antibiotics to control the inflammation.</p><p>Menstrual cycle problems after a cesarean section: Women may face a range of problems and changes in their cycle after a cesarean section, and these are the most well-known problems that may occur with the menstrual cycle after a cesarean section: 1. Delayed return of menstruation: A delay in the return of menstruation may occur after delivery, and this is normal. It may take a few months before your menstrual cycle returns to normal. 2. Bleeding: You may experience vaginal bleeding after a cesarean section. This can be temporary and disappears after the cesarean incision heals. 3. Changes in the cycle pattern: A change in the duration and amount of menstruation may occur. The cycle may be heavier or lighter than it was before delivery. 4. Pain and bloating: Some women may feel pain and bloating in the pelvic area or the cesarean area during menstruation.</p><p>In general, these changes are considered normal after a cesarean section, and they often disappear over time. However, if you are facing severe or abnormal problems in your menstrual cycle, you should go to Dr. Ahmed Marzouk, obstetrician and gynecologist, to assess your condition.</p><p>The cause of leg pain after a cesarean section: Leg pain after a cesarean section may be the result of multiple different factors that fade over time. Here are some possible causes of leg pain after a cesarean section: 1. Anesthesia and medications: During the cesarean surgery, local or general anesthesia may be used, and this can affect the blood flow and sensation in certain areas of the body, including the legs. 2. Low blood pressure: A drop in blood pressure or changes in blood flow after surgery can lead to a feeling of pain or discomfort in the legs. 3. Stress and anxiety: The stress and anxiety resulting from childbirth and the cesarean operation can affect the nervous system and cause pain in different areas of the body. 4. As a result of the cesarean operation: Cesarean operations can lead to temporary suppression of the nerves in the abdominal and pelvic area, and this may cause a feeling of tingling or pain in the legs. 5. Difficulty moving after delivery: Standing and movement may be limited at first, immediately after the cesarean section, which can lead to the accumulation of fluids in the lower limbs of the leg and cause pain. 6. Effects of breastfeeding: If you are breastfeeding naturally, you may feel pain in the legs due to the changes in hormones and fluids in the body.</p><p>Treatment of hemorrhoids after a cesarean section: Hemorrhoids are a condition resulting from the dilation of the veins in the anal and rectal area, and it is a common condition after childbirth, whether cesarean or natural. If you suffer from hemorrhoids after a cesarean section, there are some measures and treatments that can help relieve the symptoms and speed up recovery: 1. Eat a diet rich in fiber and water to prevent constipation and facilitate bowel movements. 2. Try to get enough rest and sleep, and avoid sitting for long periods. 3. Applying ice compresses to the anus can help reduce swelling and pain. 4. Topical numbing creams can be used based on the doctor''s directions to relieve pain and itching. 5. Bathing in warm water can help soothe the painful area and relieve symptoms. 6. If you suffer from constipation, you can take some laxatives. 7. Walking and exercising regularly may help improve blood circulation and thus reduce pain.</p><p>These are the most well-known risks after a cesarean section, which can affect the marital relationship after a cesarean section. Therefore, you should talk with your partner about what you are feeling so that you receive his support, especially since suturing the uterus after a cesarean section may affect your self-confidence compared to before. We also advise you to go to Dr. Ahmed Marzouk to take his advice on how to avoid mistakes after a cesarean section and how to get through this period completely safely without any risks or complications.</p>' WHERE `id`='855a89c4-9414-5b83-8b5b-4ae18ac7903e';
UPDATE `blog_posts` SET `slug_en`='baby-gender-selection-icsi', `title_en`='Baby Gender Selection with ICSI', `meta_title_en`='Baby Gender Selection | Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `meta_desc_en`='Learn how baby gender selection works with ICSI, when the sex is determined, the success rate, possible risks, and costs, with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk.', `excerpt_en`='Modern ICSI makes it possible to select your baby''s sex before pregnancy. Learn how gender selection works, when the sex is determined, its success rate, risks, and costs, with Dr. Ahmed Marzouk.', `tag_en`='Gender Selection', `image_alt_en`='Baby gender selection with ICSI showing X and Y chromosomes — Dr. Ahmed Marzouk', `body_en`='<p>ICSI may be the resort for many couples who seek to achieve pregnancy, and with increasing scientific advancement it has become possible to determine the sex of the fetus during the ICSI procedure and to determine whether it will be male or female. So how is that done? And does it affect the cost of ICSI and gender selection?</p><p>When is the sex of the fetus determined? In the natural situation, the sex of the fetus (whether male or female) is determined by the sex chromosomes it carries. Females usually carry a pair of XX sex chromosomes, while males carry a pair of XY sex chromosomes. The sex of the fetus is determined based on the sex chromosome that the fetus receives from both the father and the mother together.</p><p>Usually, the sex of the fetus appears at a late stage of pregnancy, and it is usually identified through an ultrasound examination, while it is difficult to determine the sex accurately in the early period of pregnancy.</p><p>Meanwhile, in the case of ICSI, the sex of the fetus is determined before pregnancy and before injecting the fertilized egg into the mother''s uterus.</p><p>The risks of gender selection: When the gender selection procedure is used in ICSI (PGD) to determine the sex of the fetus before implanting it in the mother''s uterus, the following possible harms of the gender selection procedure in ICSI may result: 1. Surgical risks: There may be a surgical risk associated with the gender selection procedures, such as the risk of infections, bleeding, or pain in the treated area. 2. Risk of failure: The gender selection procedure may not be 100% accurate, and an error may occur in the analysis that leads to determining the sex of the fetus incorrectly. 3. Inconclusive results: It may sometimes be difficult to obtain conclusive and accurate results, as some results may appear unclear or ambiguous, and this can create a state of uncertainty about the sex of the fetus. 4. Psychological and emotional stress: Couples who undergo the gender selection procedure may face significant psychological and emotional stress, including anxiety about the health of the fetus and the effect of the sex results on their family and personal life.</p><p>The gender selection procedure: It is a procedure during which the biological sex of the fetus is determined before implanting it in the mother''s uterus, by separating the male X and Y chromosomes from each other, then recombining the two desired chromosomes with each other to form XX if the desired sex of the fetus is female, or XY if the desired sex of the fetus is male.</p><p>Some clinics, in the regular ICSI procedure, also collect a number of eggs from the woman and fertilize them in the laboratory using sperm from the partner or a donor. Then the resulting embryos are implanted in the woman''s uterus. A small sample is taken from the embryo after fertilization and before implanting it in the mother''s uterus to determine its sex, and the decision to implant it in the uterus is made based on the results, whether it is male or female.</p><p>The success rate of the gender selection procedure: The success rate of the gender selection procedure in ICSI depends on several factors, including the technique used, the skill of the medical team performing it, the quality of the analysis itself, and the condition of the couple. The success rate may vary between different medical centers and countries, but in general the success rate of the gender selection procedure in ICSI is high in most cases, and much research indicates that success rates in gender selection ranging between 95-99% can be achieved. However, there may be some rare cases in which there is an error in the analysis or the results.</p><p>Taking into account that the success rate in gender selection may differ from the success rate of ICSI in general. ICSI may be successful in forming embryos and transferring them to the woman''s uterus, but that does not guarantee one hundred percent success in determining the sex of the fetus.</p><p>The cost of the gender selection procedure: The cost of the gender selection procedure varies based on the country, the experience of the medical team, and the technique used. The costs of gender selection may include the following: 1. Costs of medical examinations: as it requires the use of techniques such as ultrasound or DNA analysis for the medical examinations, and these costs may vary based on the price of the examination in the country and the hospital. 2. The experience and competence of the treating medical team: this may entail additional costs with the increased experience of the treating doctor and his years of experience.</p><p>That is, it is difficult to determine a specific cost for the gender selection procedure, as this depends on several factors. Therefore, it is advised to contact us to obtain accurate information about the costs associated with gender selection in the ICSI procedure.</p><p>In which month is the sex of the fetus determined? The sex of the fetus is usually determined in the later months of pregnancy, when the fetus''s external genital organs have fully formed, allowing the sex to be determined by ultrasound.</p><p>However, DNA techniques can be used to determine the sex of the fetus more accurately in the early months through DNA testing, such as amniotic fluid testing (amniocentesis), which provides accurate information about the sex.</p><p>Gender selection before pregnancy: As we mentioned, it is possible to determine the sex of the fetus before pregnancy through the ICSI technique, and this procedure is carried out by a specialized medical team. However, it should be noted that although this technique is usually accurate, it is not 100% reliable and there may be a possibility of error.</p>' WHERE `id`='7dd3709a-686a-5e53-b3f5-1efb6ffb1aed';
COMMIT;
